var e=JSON.parse("[[2801,\"Next.js is a React framework for creating fully functional web applications. It provides built-in solutions for routing, rendering on the server side and optimization. The experience of working with it includes creating pages and API routes and using the various rendering strategies.\",\"Next.js is a powerful framework on top of React, which solves many common tasks of web development out of the box.\\nThe main aspects of working with Next.js:\\n\\n- The project's structure: a clear file structure, where the pages are created in the `pages` or `app` folder (in Next.js 13+)\\n\\n- Routing: automatic routing on the basis of the file system\\n\\n- API Routes: the ability to create server-side API endpoints in the same codebase\\n\\nAn example of creating a page:\\n\\nAn example of an API route:\\n\\nConclusion: the experience of working with Next.js includes an understanding of its architecture, its built-in capabilities and the optimal practices of development.\",\"// pages/about.js\\nexport default function About() {\\n  return <h1>About us</h1>\\n}\\n\\n// pages/api/users.js\\nexport default function handler(req, res) {\\n  res.status(200).json({ users: [] })\\n}\"],[2802,\"Next.js offers three main kinds of page generation: SSG (static generation), SSR (rendering on the server) and ISR (incremental static regeneration). SSG creates the pages at build time, SSR on every request, and ISR lets you update static pages without a complete rebuild.\",\"Next.js provides flexible rendering capabilities for different scenarios of use.\\nThe types of page generation:\\n\\n- SSG (Static Site Generation)\\n\\n- The pages are generated while the application is being built\\n\\n- Ideal for content that changes rarely\\n\\n- Maximum performance and SEO-friendliness\\n\\n- SSR (Server-Side Rendering)\\n\\n- The pages are rendered on the server on every request\\n\\n- Suits dynamic content that changes often\\n\\n- Guarantees the data is current\\n\\n- ISR (Incremental Static Regeneration)\\n\\n- A hybrid approach: static pages with the ability to update\\n\\n- You can specify the interval at which the pages are regenerated\\n\\n- Ideal for content that changes often and is large in volume\\n\\nWhen to use which:\\n\\n- SSG: blogs, documentation, landing pages\\n\\n- SSR: personalized content, dynamic data\\n\\n- ISR: product catalogues, news sites\",null],[2803,\"`getStaticProps` fetches the data for static pages at build time. `getServerSideProps` fetches the data on every request, on the server. `getStaticPaths` defines which dynamic paths are to be generated in advance for static pages.\",\"These functions are the main tools for working with data in Next.js.\\nThe differences and the purposes:\\n\\n- getStaticProps\\n\\n- Used for SSG (static generation)\\n\\n- Runs while the application is being built\\n\\n- The data is cached and used for every request\\n\\n- getServerSideProps\\n\\n- Used for SSR (server-side rendering)\\n\\n- Runs on every request\\n\\n- The data is always current\\n\\n- getStaticPaths\\n\\n- Used together with `getStaticProps` for dynamic routes\\n\\n- Defines which particular paths are to be generated statically\\n\\nConclusion: the choice of function depends on the requirements for how current the data is and on the performance.\",\"export async function getStaticProps() {\\n  const data = await fetchData()\\n  return { props: { data } }\\n}\\n\\nexport async function getServerSideProps(context) {\\n  const userData = await fetchUserData(context.req)\\n  return { props: { userData } }\\n}\\n\\nexport async function getStaticPaths() {\\n  const paths = await getAllPostIds()\\n  return { paths, fallback: false }\\n}\"],[2804,\"Middleware in Next.js are functions that are run before a request is processed. They are used for authentication, logging, redirects and the modification of responses. Middleware works on edge servers and can process requests before they reach the application.\",\"Middleware provides a powerful mechanism for intercepting and processing requests before they reach the pages or the API routes.\\nThe main use cases for middleware:\\n\\n- Authentication and authorization: checking the user's access rights\\n\\n- Geolocation: redirecting users based on their location\\n\\n- Logging: gathering analytics and monitoring the requests\\n\\n- Modifying the headers: adding or changing HTTP headers\\n\\nAn example of middleware for authentication:\\n\\nThe particulars of its operation:\\n\\n- It runs on edge servers (closer to the user)\\n\\n- It can work with requests to pages and to API routes\\n\\n- It supports conditional logic on the basis of the request's path\\n\\nConclusion: middleware is a powerful tool for the centralized processing of requests and for implementing cross-cutting concerns.\",\"// middleware.js\\nimport { NextResponse } from 'next/server'\\n\\nexport function middleware(request) {\\n  const token = request.cookies.get('auth-token')\\n  \\n  if (!token) {\\n    return NextResponse.redirect(new URL('/login', request.url))\\n  }\\n  \\n  return NextResponse.next()\\n}\\n\\nexport const config = {\\n  matcher: '/dashboard/:path*'\\n}\"],[2805,\"Under SSR the HTML is formed by the server, which runs the application's code in a server-side environment. The browser receives finished HTML, shows the markup quickly, and then loads the JavaScript and “brings the page to life”, binding the handlers and the state. That gives a faster first paint and better SEO, but adds load to the server.\",\"Under SSR it matters to separate two stages: “generate the HTML” and “make the interface interactive”.\\n1) Who exactly generates the HTML\\nA definition: SSR (Server-Side Rendering) is the approach in which a page's HTML is generated on the server rather than being built entirely in the browser out of JavaScript.\\n\\n- The HTML is rendered by the server:\\n\\n- the server runs the application's code (the rendering of React components into a string of HTML, for example)\\n\\n- substitutes the data in (often obtained before the render)\\n\\n- At that stage the browser simply receives the HTML and parses it like an ordinary page.\\n\\n2) What the browser does once it has received the SSR HTML\\nThe browser first displays the page “as is”, but without full interactivity.\\n\\n- Parses the HTML, builds the DOM and displays the content.\\n\\n- Loads the application's JS bundles.\\n\\n- Performs the “bringing to life” (hydration).\\n\\nA definition: hydration is the process in which the client-side JavaScript “picks up” the HTML that has already been rendered, links it with the components and adds the event handlers.\\n3) Why SSR does not equal “everything is ready”\\nSSR speeds the appearance of the content up, but the interactivity appears once the JS has loaded.\\n\\n- Before the hydration:\\n\\n- the elements are visible\\n\\n- the handlers for clicks/inputs may not work, or may work partially (it depends on the framework and the mode)\\n\\n- After the hydration:\\n\\n- React/Next “matches” the existing DOM against the virtual tree\\n\\n- the events, the state and the effects are connected\\n\\n4) A mini-example: the idea of SSR + hydration\\n\\n5) The practical significance for the frontend\\n\\n- The pluses:\\n\\n- the content appears faster (the first paint)\\n\\n- it is indexed better (SEO)\\n\\n- The minuses:\\n\\n- more work on the server\\n\\n- caching and diagnostics are more complex\\n\\n- mismatch errors are possible, if the server and the client render differently\\n\\nConclusion: under SSR the HTML is rendered by the server. The browser then performs the hydration, so that the page becomes interactive, which is why SSR normally gives a gain in the “first show” but does not remove the need for client-side JS.\",\"// The server: generates the HTML out of the components (notionally)\\nconst html = renderToString(<App />); // An HTML string\\n// The client: \\\"brings the HTML to life\\\" (notionally)\\nhydrateRoot(document.getElementById('root'), <App />);\"],[2806,\"Server-side rendering lets you form the HTML on the server and send it to the browser ready-made. That speeds the first load of the page up and improves SEO. The user sees the content faster. Such an approach is actively used in Next.js.\",\"Under ordinary client-side rendering the browser first loads the JavaScript and then builds the interface.\\nA definition:\\nServer-Side Rendering (`SSR`) is the process of generating the HTML on the server before the page is sent to the user.\\nHow it works\\n\\n- The user makes a request.\\n\\n- The server forms the HTML.\\n\\n- The browser receives finished markup.\\n\\n- React performs the hydration.\\n\\nAn example in Next.js\\n\\nThe advantages\\n\\n- A fast first paint.\\n\\n- An improvement in SEO.\\n\\n- Suits dynamic pages.\\n\\nThe disadvantages\\n\\n- Load on the server.\\n\\n- A more complex architecture.\\n\\nConclusion\\nSSR is applied when the speed of the first load and the indexing of the pages by search systems matter.\",\"export async function getServerSideProps() {\\n  return {\\n    props: { data: \\\"example\\\" }\\n  };\\n}\"],[2807,\"In Next.js dynamic routes are created by means of files in square brackets — `[id].js`, for example. The parameter's value can be obtained through `router.query` or through the parameters of the data-loading function. Such an approach lets you create pages for different entities — users or products, for example. The routes are formed automatically on the basis of the structure of the files.\",\"Next.js uses the file system in order to define the routes, including the dynamic ones.\\nA definition:\\nA dynamic route is a route in which part of the URL is a variable and is determined at run time.\\nAn example of the structure\\n\\nSuch a file handles routes of the form:\\n\\nObtaining the parameter\\n\\nAn alternative variant (SSR or SSG)\\n\\nWhen it is used\\n\\n- Users' pages.\\n\\n- Products' pages.\\n\\n- Detail pages for content.\\n\\nConclusion\\nDynamic routes in Next.js are implemented through the file system and let you handle a URL's parameters automatically.\",\"pages/\\n  users/\\n    [id].js\\n\\n/users/1\\n/users/25\\n\\nimport { useRouter } from \\\"next/router\\\";\\n\\nconst router = useRouter();\\nconst { id } = router.query;\\n\\nexport async function getServerSideProps(context) {\\n  const { id } = context.params;\\n  return { props: { id } };\\n}\"],[2808,\"In Next.js, SSR is done by way of the server-side rendering of the page: the data is obtained on the server, and the client receives finished HTML. For SEO it matters that the key content and the metadata (title, description, canonical, OG) have already been rendered on the server. Depending on the task, SSR, SSG or ISR is chosen, so as to balance the freshness of the data against the speed. For dynamic pages SSR or ISR is often used. It also matters to configure robots/sitemap, correct canonicals and structured data where necessary.\",\"In Next.js, SEO is normally achieved not by «magic» but by the proper choice of rendering mode and careful work with the metadata.\\nA definition\\nA definition: SSR (Server-Side Rendering) is the generation of a page's HTML on the server on every request (or by a strategy), so that the user and the search engine immediately receive finished markup.\\nThe rendering strategies in Next.js and when to choose which\\n\\n- SSR\\n\\n- Needed for: personalization, data that changes often, a dependence on the request\\n\\n- SSG\\n\\n- Needed for: static pages, maximum speed, rare changes\\n\\n- ISR\\n\\n- Needed for: «almost static», but to be updated periodically without a complete deploy\\n\\nThe implementation of SSR (conceptually)\\n\\n- The data is loaded on the server\\n\\n- The server returns HTML with the content\\n\\n- The hydration happens on the client (React «brings the page to life»)\\n\\nAn example (the pages router): SSR by way of `getServerSideProps`\\n\\nSEO optimization: what you must do\\n\\n- The metadata\\n\\n- `title`, `description`\\n\\n- `canonical`\\n\\n- Open Graph / Twitter cards (for the previews)\\n\\n- Indexable content\\n\\n- The important texts and headings must be in the HTML in the server's response\\n\\n- The speed\\n\\n- The proper choice of SSR/SSG/ISR\\n\\n- The optimization of the images (next/image), the minimization of the blocking resources\\n\\n- The technical files\\n\\n- `robots.txt` and `sitemap.xml`\\n\\n- The semantics\\n\\n- Correct `h1-h2`, lists, links, alt on the images\\n\\nMetadata in Next.js (the app router)\\n\\nThe typical mistakes that break SEO\\n\\n- The content appears only after a client-side fetch (empty HTML at the start)\\n\\n- An identical `title`/`description` for every page\\n\\n- No `canonical` where URLs are duplicated (parameters/filters)\\n\\n- Rendering «everything in a div» without semantics or headings\\n\\nConclusion\\nFor SEO in Next.js the key thing is to give the search engines finished HTML with the content and correct metadata; SSR/SSG/ISR are chosen by the freshness of the data and by the requirements for speed.\",\"export async function getServerSideProps() {\\n  const res = await fetch(\\\"https://api.example.com/products\\\"); // an example\\n  const products = await res.json();\\n  return { props: { products } };\\n}\\n\\nexport const metadata = {\\n  title: \\\"A catalogue of products\\\",\\n  description: \\\"A selection of products with delivery\\\",\\n};\"],[2809,\"Server-side requests in Next.js are requests to an API or a database that are performed on the server before the HTML is sent to the client. They are used in order to return a finished page with the data to the user immediately. Such requests are inaccessible from the browser and can use secrets safely. In the app router, server-side requests are normally performed right in the server components. That improves SEO and reduces the amount of client-side JavaScript.\",\"In Next.js, requests can be performed at various stages of a page's life cycle, and server-side requests are the key mechanism for SSR, SSG and ISR.\\nA definition\\nA definition: a server-side request is a request for data that is performed in the Node.js environment (or at the edge) rather than in the user's browser.\\nWhere server-side requests are performed\\n\\n- SSR\\n\\n- On every HTTP request from the user\\n\\n- SSG\\n\\n- At build time\\n\\n- ISR\\n\\n- On a timer or at a revalidation\\n\\n- Server Components (the app router)\\n\\n- When the component is rendered on the server\\n\\nAn example of a server-side request in the app router\\n\\nThe differences from client-side requests\\n\\n- Server-side\\n\\n- No access from the browser's DevTools\\n\\n- API keys can be used\\n\\n- The data reaches the HTML immediately\\n\\n- Client-side\\n\\n- They are performed once the page has loaded\\n\\n- They are visible in the browser's network\\n\\n- They often worsen SEO when used improperly\\n\\nThe practical scenarios\\n\\n- Catalogues, articles, landing pages → server-side requests\\n\\n- Filters, interactivity, the user's actions → client-side requests\\n\\nConclusion\\nServer-side requests in Next.js are applied for the safe and SEO-friendly loading of data and are the basic tool of server-side rendering.\",\"const products = await fetch(\\\"https://api.example.com/products\\\", {\\n  cache: \\\"no-store\\\" // switches the caching off\\n}).then(r => r.json());\"],[2810,\"Server-side rendering (SSR) generates the HTML on the server and sends a finished page to the browser, which improves SEO and the time to the first paint. Client-side rendering (CSR) loads minimal HTML and JavaScript, which then builds the interface in the browser, ensuring fast transitions once it has loaded. Hydration is used in order to reconcile them, when React 'brings the static HTML received from the server to life'. The choice of approach depends on the requirements for SEO, the speed of the load and the interactivity.\",\"Server-side rendering (SSR) and client-side rendering (CSR) are two fundamental approaches to displaying web content, each with its own trade-offs. SSR means the server generates a complete HTML document for every request and sends it to the client. It is the traditional way, used in multi-page applications. CSR, characteristic of single-page applications (SPAs), loads the page's skeleton and a JavaScript bundle, which then renders the interface dynamically in the browser, managing the routing and the state on the client side.\\n\\nThe key differences and consequences\\n\\n- SEO and indexing: search bots scan HTML generated by the server (SSR) easily. Under CSR the initial HTML is often empty, which makes indexing more difficult, although modern bots can run JavaScript.\\n\\n- Perceived performance: SSR ensures the fast display of the first content (FCP), since the user sees the HTML immediately. CSR may show a loading indicator until a large JS bundle has loaded and run.\\n\\n- Interactivity: once it has loaded, CSR ensures smooth, fast transitions without reloading the page. Under SSR every navigation requires a new request to the server, although that can be optimized.\\n\\n- The load on the server: SSR creates load on the server for every render, which may require scaling. CSR shifts the computational load onto the client devices.\\n\\nModern hybrid approaches and hydration\\n\\nFrameworks such as Next.js offer hybrid models. You can use static generation (SSG) for pages that do not change often and SSR for dynamic data, for example. The key process is hydration. The server sends HTML that has been rendered in advance. Then the same React JavaScript code is loaded, which 'picks up' (hydrates) that DOM, binding the event handlers and making the page interactive.\\n\\nAn example of the code with Next.js (the App Router)\\n\\nIn this example `HomePage` is rendered on the server with the data, ensuring good SEO and a fast load. The `ClientCounter` component, marked with the 'use client' directive, is hydrated on the client and adds the interactivity.\\n\\nPractical recommendations for handling the differences\\n\\n- Use SSR or SSG for public, SEO-critical pages (landing pages, blogs, catalogues).\\n\\n- Apply CSR for admin panels or strongly interactive parts of an application, where SEO does not matter.\\n\\n- For universal applications use frameworks (Next.js, Nuxt, SvelteKit), which abstract the complexity away, letting you mix the approaches.\\n\\n- Take the \\\"flicker\\\" at hydration into account: if the client's initial state (the theme, for example) does not match the server's render, the page may flash. The solution is synchronization through scripts or holding the state in cookies.\\n\\n- Avoid direct manipulations of the DOM in components that may be rendered on the server; use hooks of the `useEffect` kind or the check `typeof window !== 'undefined'`.\\n\\nConclusion: the choice between SSR and CSR is a compromise between the speed of the initial load, SEO and rich interactivity. The hybrid approaches with hydration provided by the modern meta-frameworks let you get the best of both worlds: a fast initial render for SEO and the subsequent smooth operation of an SPA.\",\"// app/page.js — A server component by default in Next.js 13+\\nexport default async function HomePage() {\\n  // The data is requested on the server\\n  const data = await fetchData();\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>The home page (SSR)</h1>\\n      <p>The data: {data.message}</p>\\n      {/* A client component for the interactivity */}\\n      <ClientCounter />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// app/components/ClientCounter.js\\n'use client'; // Indicates that this is a client component\\nimport { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nexport default function ClientCounter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>The counter: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Increase</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2811,\"The hydration of state is the process of passing data from the server to the client, in order to avoid repeated requests. The server renders the page with the initial data and embeds that data in the HTML — in a `<script>` tag, for example. On the client the application reads that state and uses it as its initial state instead of making a new request. That speeds the first paint up and avoids the interface flickering.\",\"The hydration of state is a key technique in the architecture of modern web applications, especially ones that use server-side rendering (SSR) or static generation (SSG). Its aim is to avoid the situation in which the client application, having received static HTML from the server, is forced to request the same data again through an API, which leads to delays and to the interface flickering.\\n\\nHow it works\\n\\nThe process can be divided into several stages:\\n\\n- The server-side preparation: the application's code runs on the server (in React with Next.js, or Nuxt.js for Vue, for example). The application requests the data it needs (from a database or an API) and renders the components into HTML using that state.\\n\\n- Embedding the state: the resulting state (normally a JavaScript object) is serialized into a JSON string and embedded directly in the HTML document sent to the client. Most often that is done inside a `<script>` tag with a particular identifier.\\n\\n- The client-side hydration: the browser receives the HTML and displays it (a fast paint), then downloads the application's JavaScript bundle. The application's code 'mounts' onto the DOM structure that already exists. At that stage it reads the embedded state from the `<script>` tag and uses it as its initial state, instead of initializing the state as empty or making a new request.\\n\\nA practical example (Next.js with React)\\n\\nIn Next.js the `getServerSideProps` or `getStaticProps` method is often used for this.\\n\\nOn the client the framework will pick that data up automatically. In other ecosystems (Vue with Nuxt, for example) the principle is the same, but the implementation can differ in its syntax.\\n\\nWhere and why it is used\\n\\nThis approach is critically important for:\\n\\n- Performance: it removes an unnecessary network request, speeding the time to interactivity up.\\n\\n- SEO: search bots see a fully formed page with its data.\\n\\n- The user experience: it rules out flicker or a loading state when moving between an SPA's pages.\\n\\nConclusion: the hydration of state is worth applying in any application where the speed of the first load and SEO matter, especially in online shops, media sites and catalogues, where the initial data is known at the moment the page is rendered.\",\"// The page pages/product/[id].js\\nexport async function getServerSideProps(context) {\\n  const { id } = context.params;\\n  // The data request on the server\\n  const res = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/products/${id}`);\\n  const product = await res.json();\\n\\n  // We return the props, which will reach the page\\n  return {\\n    props: {\\n      product // This state will be passed to the client\\n    },\\n  };\\n}\\n\\n// The page component will receive product as a prop\\nfunction ProductPage({ product }) {\\n  // On the client product is already available without a request\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>{product.name}</h1>\\n      <p>{product.description}</p>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// Next.js serializes the `product` object automatically\\n// and inserts it into the HTML inside __NEXT_DATA__.\"],[2812,\"Next.js is a framework based on React, which adds server-side rendering (SSR), static generation (SSG), routing based on the file system and API routes. Unlike pure React, which is only a client-side library for the UI, Next.js provides a ready-made architecture for full-stack applications. It makes SEO easier, improves performance by way of loading the data in advance, and optimizes the bundles automatically.\",\"The purpose of Next.js\\nNext.js is a production-ready framework for React, which solves the tasks that lie beyond the scope of a client-side library. It provides built-in solutions for server-side rendering (SSR), static generation (SSG), incremental static regeneration (ISR), routing, the optimization of images and API routes. That lets you create both full-fledged web applications with server-side logic and high-performance static sites.\\nThe key differences from pure React\\n\\n- Routing: in React, React Router is used, which works on the client. Next.js uses the file system in order to define the routes (pages/ or app/), which makes the navigation easier and supports dynamic paths.\\n- Rendering: React renders everything on the client (CSR). Next.js lets you choose between CSR, SSR, SSG and ISR for every page, which improves SEO and the loading time.\\n- API routes: Next.js lets you create server-side endpoints inside the project (pages/api), which removes the need for a separate backend for simple tasks.\\n- Optimization: the built-in optimization of images, fonts and scripts, and automatic code splitting — in React that requires additional libraries.\\nAn example of the code: a page with SSR in Next.js\\n\\nIn this example the data is loaded on the server before the HTML is sent to the client, which improves SEO and the time to the first content (FCP). In pure React you would have to use useEffect and handle the loading state.\\nConclusion\\nNext.js is worth applying when SEO optimization, the fast loading of pages, built-in server-side logic or the static generation of content is required. For simple single-page applications with no need for SEO, pure React can be the lighter solution.\",\"// pages/posts/[id].js\\nexport async function getServerSideProps(context) {\\n  const { id } = context.params;\\n  const res = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/posts/${id}`);\\n  const post = await res.json();\\n  return { props: { post } };\\n}\\n\\nexport default function Post({ post }) {\\n  return <div><h1>{post.title}</h1><p>{post.body}</p></div>;\\n}\"],[2813,\"Routing in Next.js is based on the file system. Every file in the pages or app folders automatically becomes a route. The file pages/about.js corresponds to the path /about, for example. Dynamic routes are supported by way of square brackets — [id].js, for example. In version 13 the App Router appeared, with support for nested layouts and server components.\",\"The basics of routing in Next.js\\nNext.js uses the file system in order to define the routes. That means the structure of the folders and files in the `pages` (Pages Router) or `app` (App Router) directories maps directly onto the application's URLs. Such an approach makes creating and maintaining the navigation easier, since the developer does not have to configure the routes by hand.\\nThe Pages Router (the classic approach)\\nIn the Pages Router every JavaScript or TypeScript file in the `pages` folder becomes a route. For example:\\n\\nDynamic segments are denoted with square brackets — `[id].js`, for example. The parameters are accessed through the `useRouter` hook or through `getServerSideProps`.\\nThe App Router (the new approach with Next.js 13)\\nThe App Router uses the `app` folder and supports nested layouts, server components and improved performance. The structure is similar, but special files are added:\\n\\nThe `layout.js` file defines the wrapper for a group of pages, which lets you reuse the interface (the header and the footer, for example).\\nA practical example\\nLet us create a simple blog with dynamic routes:\\n\\nNow the URL `/blog/my-post` will display the heading \\\"Post: my-post\\\", and `/blog` the blog's list.\\nConclusion\\nNext.js's file-based routing speeds development up, making the navigation intuitive and predictable. It is especially useful for projects with a clear hierarchy of pages, such as blogs, documentation or online shops.\",\"pages/\\n  index.js       -> /\\n  about.js       -> /about\\n  blog/\\n    index.js     -> /blog\\n    [slug].js    -> /blog/:slug\\n\\napp/\\n  page.js        -> /\\n  layout.js      -> the common layout for every child route\\n  about/\\n    page.js      -> /about\\n  blog/\\n    [slug]/\\n      page.js    -> /blog/:slug\\n\\n// app/blog/[slug]/page.js\\nexport default function BlogPost({ params }) {\\n  return <h1>Post: {params.slug}</h1>;\\n}\\n\\n// app/blog/page.js\\nexport default function BlogList() {\\n  return <h1>Blog list</h1>;\\n}\"],[2814,\"The waterfall problem arises when data requests are performed sequentially, waiting for the previous one to finish, which increases the total loading time. In Next.js that often happens when using async/await in getServerSideProps or in server components without parallelization. To avoid it, you have to start the independent requests at the same time by means of Promise.all or parallel calls.\",\"What is the waterfall problem?\\nThe waterfall problem (or cascade problem) in Next.js arises when several requests for data are performed sequentially rather than in parallel. Every subsequent request waits for the previous one to finish, which leads to the waiting times adding up. That is especially critical on the server side, where the delay of every request increases the time it takes to render the page.\\nAn example of the problem\\nLet us look at the typical case in getServerSideProps:\\n\\nThe total time is 600 ms, although the requests are independent.\\nHow to avoid it\\nUse Promise.all for the parallel execution of independent requests:\\n\\nNow all three requests are performed at the same time, and the total time is equal to the longest of them (200 ms).\\nWhere else it is met with\\nA waterfall can arise in React server components when using async/await inside components that depend on one another. In such cases, move the independent requests up to the top level or use Suspense for streaming the load.\\nConclusion: the waterfall problem worsens the performance of Next.js applications. It is avoided by the parallelization of independent requests through Promise.all or similar mechanisms, which matters especially for server-side rendering and for improving the user experience.\",\"export async function getServerSideProps() {\\n  const user = await fetch('/api/user'); // 200ms\\n  const posts = await fetch('/api/posts'); // waits for user, another 200ms\\n  const comments = await fetch('/api/comments'); // waits for posts, another 200ms\\n  return { props: { user, posts, comments } };\\n}\\n\\nexport async function getServerSideProps() {\\n  const [user, posts, comments] = await Promise.all([\\n    fetch('/api/user'),\\n    fetch('/api/posts'),\\n    fetch('/api/comments')\\n  ]);\\n  return { props: { user, posts, comments } };\\n}\"],[2815,\"Server components are rendered on the server, require no JavaScript on the client and cannot use hooks or event handlers. Client components are rendered on the client, support interactivity and use hooks. Hydration is the process in which React adds interactivity to the static HTML sent from the server, connecting the event handlers and the state.\",\"The main differences between server and client components\\nIn the Next.js App Router, components are server components by default. Server components run on the server, generate static HTML and send no JavaScript to the client. They are ideal for displaying data that requires no interactivity — content from a database, for example. Client components, by contrast, run in the browser and can use React's hooks (useState, useEffect), event handlers and the browser's APIs.\\nWhat is hydration?\\nHydration is the process in which React takes the static HTML sent from the server and turns it into an interactive application. React adds the event handlers, restores the state and connects the virtual DOM. That lets you improve the performance of the load, since the user sees the content before the JavaScript has fully loaded.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse server components for static content and data, and client ones for interactive elements. Hydration makes the server-side HTML interactive automatically, which improves the user experience and SEO.\",\"// A server component (the default)\\nexport default function ServerComponent() {\\n  const data = fetchDataFromDB(); // runs on the server\\n  return <div>{data}</div>;\\n}\\n\\n// A client component\\n'use client';\\nimport { useState } from 'react';\\nexport default function ClientComponent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  return <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>{count}</button>;\\n}\"],[2816,\"Hydration is the process in which React takes the HTML that has already been rendered on the server and «brings it to life», connecting the event handlers and the state. That lets the page be interactive without a complete repaint. In Next.js the hydration happens automatically once the JS bundle has loaded.\",\"What is hydration?\\nHydration is a key mechanism in React and Next.js, which links server-side rendering (SSR) with client-side rendering. When the server sends the HTML markup, it already contains all of the content, but it is not interactive. Hydration is the process in which React «brings that static markup to life», binding the virtual tree of components, the state and the event handlers to it.\\nHow does it work?\\nIn Next.js, on the first request the server generates the HTML and sends it to the client. The browser displays the content instantly, which improves SEO and the time to the first byte. Then the JavaScript bundle is loaded and React performs the hydration: it matches the existing DOM against the virtual tree and adds the interactivity. If the markup matches, React does not repaint the page but merely connects the event listeners.\\n\\nWhy does that matter?\\nHydration lets you combine the advantages of SSR (a fast first response, SEO) with those of an SPA (interactivity, smooth transitions). Without it the page would be static; with it, a full-fledged application. In Next.js the hydration is automatic, but it matters to avoid divergences between the server-side and the client-side rendering, or React may issue warnings.\\nConclusion\\nHydration is the bridge between server-side and client-side rendering. It is necessary for creating fast, interactive applications on Next.js. An understanding of this process helps you avoid errors when working with SSR and optimize the performance.\",\"// An example: a component with useState\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  return <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>{count}</button>\\n}\\n\\n// The server renders the HTML: <button>0</button>\\n// After the hydration React adds the onClick and the state\"],[2817,\"Client components in Next.js are rendered on the server in order to generate the initial HTML, and are then hydrated in the browser. That means their HTML markup is created on the server, but the interactivity and the handling of events work only once the JavaScript has loaded in the browser.\",\"How client components work in Next.js\\nIn Next.js, client components are the components marked with the `'use client'` directive. They take part in the server-side rendering: the server generates their HTML markup, which is sent to the client. That matters for SEO and for a fast initial load, since the user sees the content before the JavaScript has run.\\nOnce the HTML has loaded in the browser, Next.js performs the hydration: the component's JavaScript code is loaded and bound to the existing markup, adding the interactivity (the event handlers, the state, the effects). Before the hydration the client component is displayed as static HTML.\\nAn example\\n\\nThis component is rendered on the server as `<button>Count: 0</button>`, and then becomes interactive in the browser.\\nConclusion\\nClient components do not run only in the browser — they are rendered on the server for the initial HTML, but their logic and interactivity work exclusively on the client. That combination ensures a fast load and full-fledged interaction.\",\"// app/counter.tsx\\n'use client';\\nimport { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nexport default function Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  return (\\n    <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>\\n      Count: {count}\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2818,\"If you place a lot of client components in the root layout, the whole layout will become a client one. That will increase the size of the JavaScript bundle, slow the initial load down and worsen SEO, since the server-side rendering will be limited. It is better to keep the layout a server one and to move the client components closer to the place where they are used.\",\"What happens when 'use client' is used in a layout\\nIn Next.js (the App Router) the root layout is a server component by default. When you add client components with the 'use client' directive to it, the whole layout and all of its child components become part of the client bundle. That means the JavaScript for those components will be sent to the browser, increasing the size of the code that is downloaded.\\nThe effect on performance\\nA large number of client components in the layout leads to the following problems:\\n\\n- An increase in the initial loading time (TTI) because of the greater volume of JS.\\n- A reduction in the speed of indexing by search systems, since part of the content is rendered on the client.\\n- A complication of the caching and the optimization on the server.\\nAn example\\n\\nInstead of that it is better to leave the layout a server one and to move the client components into the particular pages or child components where they really are needed.\\nConclusion\\nUse 'use client' only where interactivity is required (event handlers, state hooks). Keep the layout and the static parts server-side, so as to minimize the bundle and improve the performance.\",\"// app/layout.tsx\\nimport ClientComponent from './ClientComponent';\\n\\nexport default function RootLayout({ children }) {\\n  return (\\n    <html>\\n      <body>\\n        <ClientComponent /> {/* makes the layout a client one */}\\n        {children}\\n      </body>\\n    </html>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2819,\"Yes, there are such situations. If the application is an internal tool, an admin panel or a dashboard with closed access, where SEO does not matter and the users are already authorized, an ordinary SPA on React will be simpler and cheaper. Next.js adds complexity with server-side rendering, deployment and configuration, which is not justified without public content and requirements for the speed of the first load.\",\"When Next.js is excessive\\nNext.js is a framework based on React, which adds server-side rendering (SSR), static generation (SSG) and other capabilities. Those functions are not always needed, however. If your application is an internal control panel, an analytics dashboard or a tool for employees, where the access is restricted by authorization, the search systems do not index the pages and the users do not depend on SEO. In that case a classic SPA on React with Create React App or Vite will be simpler: fewer dependencies, a faster build, a simpler deploy to static hosting.\\nThe key criteria for the choice\\n\\n- Public content and SEO — Next.js is needed.\\n- A high speed of the first load for marketing pages — SSG is useful.\\n- Frequently updated data and dynamics — an SPA can be more convenient.\\n- The team and the infrastructure — if there is no experience with server-side code, an SPA lowers the threshold of entry.\\nAn example: when an SPA is enough\\n\\nHere there is no need for SSR, because the data comes after the login and SEO is not required. Such an application can be deployed on Netlify or Vercel as static files.\\nConclusion\\nUse Next.js when SEO, the fast delivery of public content or isomorphic applications are needed. For internal tools, admin panels and applications with closed access, an ordinary SPA on React is the more rational choice: it is simpler to develop and to maintain.\",\"// A React SPA (Vite) - a simple application for internal use\\nimport React, { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Dashboard() {\\n  const [data, setData] = useState([]);\\n  // loading the data from the API after the authorization\\n  return <div>The control panel</div>;\\n}\\n\\nexport default Dashboard;\"],[2820,\"In Next.js data can be loaded on the server in Server Components, using async/await, or on the client in Client Components by means of the useEffect hook or libraries. For caching, fetch with the cache and next.revalidate options is applied, as are static generation (SSG) and incremental regeneration (ISR).\",\"In Next.js the loading of data depends on where the code runs: on the server or on the client. Server Components run only on the server, which lets you address databases or APIs directly without additional layers. Client Components work in the browser and require different approaches — the use of hooks or of libraries for managing state, for example.\\nLoading data in server components\\nIn Server Components you can use async functions and await in order to obtain the data. That simplifies the code, since there is no need to handle the loading and error states on the client. An example:\\n\\nBy default, fetch in Next.js caches the results, which speeds repeated requests up. You can control the caching by means of the options.\\nLoading data in client components\\nFor Client Components, which are marked with the 'use client' directive, the loading of data normally happens in a useEffect or by means of libraries such as SWR or TanStack Query. That is necessary when the data depends on the user's actions or requires interactivity.\\n\\nThe caching strategies\\nNext.js offers several caching strategies for optimizing the performance:\\n\\n- Static generation (SSG) — the data is loaded once at the build and cached. Suits content that changes rarely.\\n- Incremental static regeneration (ISR) — lets you update static pages in the background at a given interval of time, using the option `next: { revalidate: 60 }`.\\n- Dynamic loading — if the data has to be fresh at all times, the caching can be switched off by setting `cache: 'no-store'` in the fetch.\\nAn example of using ISR:\\n\\nThe choice of strategy depends on the frequency with which the data is updated and on the requirements for how current it is. For static content it is better to use SSG; for data that changes often, dynamic loading or ISR.\\nIn summary: an understanding of the differences between server and client components, and the ability to apply the caching strategies, lets you create fast, scalable applications on Next.js, minimizing the load on the server and improving the user experience.\",\"// app/page.tsx (a Server Component)\\nexport default async function Page() {\\n  const res = await fetch('https://api.example.com/data');\\n  const data = await res.json();\\n  return <div>{data.title}</div>;\\n}\\n\\n// app/dashboard.tsx (a Client Component)\\n'use client';\\nimport { useEffect, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nexport default function Dashboard() {\\n  const [data, setData] = useState(null);\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    fetch('/api/dashboard')\\n      .then(res => res.json())\\n      .then(setData);\\n  }, []);\\n  return <div>{data?.message}</div>;\\n}\\n\\nconst res = await fetch('https://api.example.com/posts', {\\n  next: { revalidate: 60 } // update every 60 seconds\\n});\"],[2821,\"Redux helps you manage an application's state by holding it in one place (a global store). It works with React through context and providers, giving the components access to the shared state. React-Redux makes using Redux in React easier by providing hooks such as `useSelector` and `useDispatch`.\",\"Redux is a library for managing state, designed to make working with state in complex applications easier. It offers a single place in which to hold the state (a global store), which makes passing data between components easier without having to drill it through a multitude of levels.\\nRedux consists of three key elements:\\n\\n- The store: this is the object that contains all of the application's state.\\n\\n- The actions: these are objects describing what is to change in the state. They always contain the type of the action (`type`) and may contain additional data (`payload`).\\n\\n- The reducers: these are functions that take the current state and an action and return a new state.\\n\\nIt integrates with React through the React-Redux library, which provides:\\n\\n- `Provider`: the component that makes the store available to the whole tree of React components.\\n\\n- The `useSelector` and `useDispatch` hooks: for obtaining data from the state and for dispatching actions respectively.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nRedux is useful in large applications with a multitude of pieces of state that have to be synchronized between the components. For small projects, however, it can be excessively complex. Alternatives such as React Context or Zustand can be simpler in some cases.\",\"// The action\\nconst increment = { type: 'INCREMENT' };\\n// The reducer\\nfunction counterReducer(state = 0, action) {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'INCREMENT':\\n      return state + 1;\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n}\\n// Creating the store\\nimport { createStore } from 'redux';\\nconst store = createStore(counterReducer);\\n// The React component\\nimport React from 'react';\\nimport { Provider, useSelector, useDispatch } from 'react-redux';\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const count = useSelector((state) => state);\\n  const dispatch = useDispatch();\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => dispatch(increment)}>Increment</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <Provider store={store}>\\n      <Counter />\\n    </Provider>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2822,\"The key principles of Redux:\\n\\n- A single store: all of the state is held in one object.\\n\\n- The state is read-only: the state can be changed only by way of actions.\\n\\n- The changes are made by pure functions: reducers are pure functions, which take the current state and an action and return a new state.\",\"Redux is founded on three key principles:\\n\\n- A Single Source of Truth:\\nAll of the application's state is held in one object, called the store. That makes debugging easier, since all of the application's data is in one place. For example:\\n\\n- The State is Read-Only:\\nThe state must not be changed directly. Instead, actions are created, which describe the changes. That makes the changes predictable and easy to track. For example:\\n\\n- Changes are Made with Pure Functions:\\nReducers are used in order to handle the actions and change the state — functions that have no side effects and always return the same value for the same input data. An example of a reducer:\\n\\nThese principles make Redux predictable and convenient for working in a team. If the application behaves incorrectly, for example, you can easily trace which action caused the changes and restore the previous state.\",\"const store = {\\n  user: { name: 'Alice', loggedIn: true },\\n  cart: [{ id: 1, name: 'Item A', quantity: 2 }]\\n};\\n\\nconst action = { type: 'ADD_ITEM', payload: { id: 2, name: 'Item B' } };\\n\\nfunction cartReducer(state = [], action) {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'ADD_ITEM':\\n      return [...state, action.payload];\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n}\"],[2823,\"Redux is convenient for working with global state in large applications, where the data has to be available in different parts of the tree of components. It makes managing the state easier, eliminates \\\"prop drilling\\\" and makes the changes predictable. Components' local state is simpler, but it is hard to scale if the data is needed in several places in the application.\",\"Redux has several advantages in comparison with local state, especially in complex applications:\\n\\n- A Single Source of Truth:\\nIn Redux all of the application's state is held in one place. That makes debugging easier, since you can quickly understand how the state changes by looking through the actions and the reducers. Local state, by contrast, is held separately in every component, which makes managing the data more complex.\\n\\n- The elimination of prop drilling:\\nWhen data has to be passed through several levels of components, that causes difficulties. Redux lets the components obtain the state they need directly through `useSelector` or `connect`, without having to drill props through the intermediate levels.\\n\\n- Support for predictability:\\nRedux makes the changes of state predictable thanks to the principle of immutability and a clear structure (actions -> reducers -> a new state). Local state can be changed directly, which makes tracking the source of a change more difficult.\\n\\n- Tools for debugging:\\nRedux DevTools let you track easily which actions have happened and go back to a previous state, which makes development considerably easier.\\n\\nAn example of the comparison:\\n\\nRedux is especially useful in large applications with multi-component dependencies. But in simple applications local state can be more convenient and require no additional configuration.\",\"// Local state\\nfunction ParentComponent() {\\n  const [data, setData] = useState(\\\"Initial Data\\\");\\n  return <ChildComponent data={data} setData={setData} />;\\n}\\n// Redux\\nimport { useSelector, useDispatch } from 'react-redux';\\nfunction Component() {\\n  const data = useSelector((state) => state.data);\\n  const dispatch = useDispatch();\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>{data}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: \\\"UPDATE_DATA\\\", payload: \\\"New Data\\\" })}>\\n        Update Data\\n      </button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2824,\"Immutability means that Redux's state is never changed directly — a new state is created instead. That lets you track the changes easily, inspect previous versions of the data and avoid the errors associated with unexpected modifications of the state\",\"Immutability is a key principle of Redux, which implies that the state must not be changed directly. Instead, when the data is updated, a new state object is created. That has several advantages:\\n\\n- Predictability:\\nIf the state remains unchanged, the changes always happen in the same way, by way of the reducers. That makes testing and debugging easier.\\n\\n- A history of the changes:\\nThanks to immutability you can easily save previous states and go back to them, which is used in Redux DevTools, for example.\\n\\n- Avoiding unexpected changes:\\nWhen the state is changed directly, that can lead to errors that are hard to detect. When working with a reference type (objects or arrays), for example, changing the state in one place may affect another place by accident. Immutability eliminates that problem.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nRedux uses the Immer library in order to make working with immutability easier, which lets you write code in a \\\"mutable\\\" style while preserving immutability:\\n\\nImmutability makes Redux a powerful tool for scalable applications, since it lets you keep control over the state and avoid unexpected side effects.\",\"// A violation of the principle of immutability\\nfunction reducer(state = { count: 0 }, action) {\\n  if (action.type === 'INCREMENT') {\\n    state.count++; // Changing the original object\\n    return state;  // Wrong\\n  }\\n  return state;\\n}\\n// Observing the principle of immutability\\nfunction reducer(state = { count: 0 }, action) {\\n  if (action.type === 'INCREMENT') {\\n    return { ...state, count: state.count + 1 }; // Creating a new object\\n  }\\n  return state;\\n}\\n\\nimport produce from 'immer';\\nconst reducer = produce((draft, action) => {\\n  if (action.type === 'INCREMENT') {\\n    draft.count++;\\n  }\\n});\"],[2825,\"Pure functions are functions that always return the same value for the same input data and have no side effects. In Redux, reducers have to be pure functions, so that the changes of state are predictable and easy to test.\",\"Pure functions are functions that satisfy two main criteria:\\n\\n- Determinacy: for the same input data the result is always the same.\\n\\n- The absence of side effects: the function does not change external data and does not call external actions such as requests to an API or changes to the DOM.\\n\\nIn Redux, reducers are pure functions, which are responsible for updating the application's state. They take the current state and an action and return a new state without changing the existing one. That lets you:\\n\\n- Guarantee the predictability of the changes of state.\\n\\n- Test the reducers easily, by checking that for a given input they always return the expected output.\\n\\n- Work with tools such as Redux DevTools, in order to track and replay the changes of state.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe purity of the reducers makes working in team development easier and helps you observe Redux's main principles. Side effects (such as API requests), for example, are moved out into middleware — `redux-thunk` or `redux-saga`, for example.\",\"// A pure function: predictable behaviour and no side effects\\nfunction counterReducer(state = 0, action) {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'INCREMENT':\\n      return state + 1;\\n    case 'DECREMENT':\\n      return state - 1;\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n}\\n// An impure function: causes side effects\\nfunction impureReducer(state = 0, action) {\\n  if (action.type === 'INCREMENT') {\\n    console.log('Incrementing count'); // A side effect\\n    return state + 1;\\n  }\\n  return state;\\n}\"],[2826,\"The flow of data in Redux is one-directional. The components dispatch actions, which are passed into the reducers through the store. The reducers update the state, and the components receive the updates through their subscriptions.\",\"The flow of data in Redux represents a one-directional architecture, which makes it simple and predictable. Here is the typical cycle:\\n\\n- The action:\\nA component initiates an action — an object describing what is to happen. For example:\\n\\n- Dispatching the action:\\nThe action is passed into the store by means of the `dispatch` method:\\n\\n- The reducer:\\nThe store passes the action into the reducers, which take the current state and the action and return a new state:\\n\\n- Updating the state:\\nThe store saves the new state and notifies the subscribed components that it has changed.\\n\\n- The display in the component:\\nThe components subscribed to the state through `useSelector` or `connect` receive the new value and are repainted:\\n\\nAn example of the complete cycle:\\n\\nSuch a one-directional structure makes the flow of data easier to understand and lets you avoid tangled interrelations between the components. It also makes Redux convenient for working in large teams.\",\"const incrementAction = { type: 'INCREMENT' };\\n\\nstore.dispatch(incrementAction);\\n\\nfunction counterReducer(state = 0, action) {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'INCREMENT':\\n      return state + 1;\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst count = useSelector((state) => state.count);\\n\\nimport { createStore } from 'redux';\\nimport { Provider, useDispatch, useSelector } from 'react-redux';\\n// The reducer\\nfunction counterReducer(state = 0, action) {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'INCREMENT':\\n      return state + 1;\\n    case 'DECREMENT':\\n      return state - 1;\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n}\\n// The store\\nconst store = createStore(counterReducer);\\n// The components\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const count = useSelector((state) => state);\\n  const dispatch = useDispatch();\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: 'INCREMENT' })}>Increment</button>\\n      <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: 'DECREMENT' })}>Decrement</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n// The application\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <Provider store={store}>\\n      <Counter />\\n    </Provider>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2827,\"`connect` links React components with Redux's state and actions. It takes two functions: `mapStateToProps` for connecting the state and `mapDispatchToProps` for passing the actions into the component. As a result the component gains access to the data it needs and to the ability to change the state.\",\"The `connect` function from the React Redux library is a HOC (Higher-Order Component), which links a component with the Redux store. It lets you pass Redux's state and actions into a component through props.\\n`connect` takes two main parameters:\\n\\n- `mapStateToProps`:\\nThis is a function that determines which data from the Redux state is to be passed into the component. It takes the store's current state as its argument and returns an object, which will be added to the component's props.\\n\\n- `mapDispatchToProps`:\\nThis is a function that passes the methods for dispatching actions (`dispatch`) into the component. It can be a function or an object:\\n\\nAn example of its use:\\n\\nIn modern React the `useSelector` and `useDispatch` hooks are used more often, which perform the same functions as `connect` but by way of functional components. `connect` nonetheless remains useful, especially in class components.\",\"const mapStateToProps = (state) => ({\\n  count: state.counter\\n});\\n\\nconst mapDispatchToProps = (dispatch) => ({\\n  increment: () => dispatch({ type: 'INCREMENT' }),\\n});\\n\\nimport React from 'react';\\nimport { connect } from 'react-redux';\\nfunction Counter({ count, increment }) {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={increment}>Increment</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\nconst mapStateToProps = (state) => ({\\n  count: state.counter,\\n});\\nconst mapDispatchToProps = (dispatch) => ({\\n  increment: () => dispatch({ type: 'INCREMENT' }),\\n});\\nexport default connect(mapStateToProps, mapDispatchToProps)(Counter);\"],[2828,\"`dispatch` is the function that sends actions into the Redux store. Those actions are handled by the reducers, which update the state. `dispatch` is used in order to interact with the global state and to cause changes.\",\"`dispatch` is the central method in Redux, which lets you initiate changes of state. It takes an action object and passes it into the store, where the reducers handle that action and return an updated state.\\nAn example of an action:\\n\\nWhen `dispatch` is called, the following happens:\\n\\n- The action is passed into the store.\\n\\n- The store sends the action to every reducer.\\n\\n- The reducers update the state on the basis of the action's type.\\n\\n- The components subscribed to the store receive the updated state and are repainted.\\n\\nIn React Redux, `dispatch` can be used directly through the `useDispatch` hook:\\n\\nOr through the `mapDispatchToProps` function with `connect`:\\n\\n`dispatch` also supports middleware such as `redux-thunk` or `redux-saga`, which lets you dispatch asynchronous actions (API requests, for example) or handle complex chains of actions. That makes `dispatch` a universal tool for managing an application's state.\",\"const incrementAction = { type: 'INCREMENT' };\\nstore.dispatch(incrementAction);\\n\\nimport { useDispatch } from 'react-redux';\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const dispatch = useDispatch();\\n  return (\\n    <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: 'INCREMENT' })}>\\n      Increment\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nconst mapDispatchToProps = (dispatch) => ({\\n  increment: () => dispatch({ type: 'INCREMENT' }),\\n});\"],[2829,\"Container components are React components that link to the Redux store in order to manage state. They receive the data from the state through `mapStateToProps` and pass the actions through `mapDispatchToProps`. As a result they work as intermediaries between Redux and the presentational components.\",\"The concept of container components in React Redux is founded on a separation of responsibility:\\n\\n- Containers are concentrated on the connection to the Redux store. They receive the data from the global state and pass it to their child components.\\n\\n- Presentational components are responsible for displaying the data and do not interact with Redux directly. They receive the data through props and call functions in order to dispatch actions.\\n\\nContainers are often created by means of the `connect` function, which links a component with the state and the actions. An example:\\n\\nContainers make the reuse of logic easier, since they isolate the work with Redux. In modern React the `useSelector` and `useDispatch` hooks are popular, which perform the same task but remove the need to create separate containers:\\n\\nSo container components are convenient for managing state, but their concept is becoming less relevant thanks to the modern hooks.\",\"import React from 'react';\\nimport { connect } from 'react-redux';\\nfunction Counter({ count, increment }) {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={increment}>Increment</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n// Linking with the state and the actions\\nconst mapStateToProps = (state) => ({ count: state.counter });\\nconst mapDispatchToProps = (dispatch) => ({\\n  increment: () => dispatch({ type: 'INCREMENT' }),\\n});\\n// Exporting the container\\nexport default connect(mapStateToProps, mapDispatchToProps)(Counter);\\n\\nimport React from 'react';\\nimport { useSelector, useDispatch } from 'react-redux';\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const count = useSelector((state) => state.counter);\\n  const dispatch = useDispatch();\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: 'INCREMENT' })}>Increment</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2830,\"In Redux, actions describe what is to happen, reducers determine how the state changes in response to the actions, and the store manages all of the application's state. These three elements work together in order to sustain a predictable flow of data.\",\"Redux is built on three main elements: actions, reducers and the store. Each of them performs its own unique role.\\nActions:\\nAn action is an object that describes what is to happen in the application. It always contains a `type` field (the type of the action), which determines which operation is to be performed. An action can also contain additional data (`payload`):\\n\\nReducers:\\nA reducer is a pure function that takes the current state and an action and returns a new state. It updates only the part of the state for which it is responsible:\\n\\nThe store:\\nThe store manages the state of the whole application. It is created by means of the `createStore` function, which takes the reducers and returns an object with methods for working with the state:\\n\\nThe flow of data in Redux looks like this:\\n\\n- A component dispatches an action through `dispatch`.\\n\\n- The action is passed to the reducers through the store.\\n\\n- The reducers return an updated state.\\n\\n- The store notifies the subscribed components of the new data.\\n\\nAn example of the complete cycle:\\n\\nThe joint work of the actions, the reducers and the store ensures a clear architecture and the predictability of the changes of state in the application. That makes Redux a powerful tool for managing state in large applications.\",\"const incrementAction = { type: 'INCREMENT' };\\nconst addTodoAction = { type: 'ADD_TODO', payload: { text: 'Learn Redux' } };\\n\\nfunction counterReducer(state = 0, action) {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'INCREMENT':\\n      return state + 1;\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nimport { createStore } from 'redux';\\n\\nconst store = createStore(counterReducer);\\nconsole.log(store.getState()); // Obtaining the current state\\nstore.dispatch({ type: 'INCREMENT' }); // Dispatching an action\\n\\n// The reducer\\nfunction todoReducer(state = [], action) {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'ADD_TODO':\\n      return [...state, action.payload];\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n}\\n// The store\\nconst store = createStore(todoReducer);\\n// Dispatching the action\\nstore.dispatch({ type: 'ADD_TODO', payload: { text: 'Learn Redux' } });\\nconsole.log(store.getState()); // [{ text: 'Learn Redux' }]\"],[2831,\"Actions in Redux are ordinary JavaScript objects, which describe what is to happen in the application. They necessarily contain a `type` field, which determines the type of the action, and may include additional data in a `payload` field. Actions are used in order to initiate changes of state by way of the reducers.\",\"Actions are the key elements in Redux, which describe an intention to change the application's state. They are passed through the `dispatch` function and handled by the reducers.\\nThe minimal structure of an action:\\n\\n`type`: the `type` field is mandatory; it determines the type of the action and tells the reducers how to handle that action.\\n`payload`: additional data, which is passed along with the action. That can be parameters, values or other particulars needed in order to update the state.\\n\\nAn example of using an action:\\nDispatching the action:\\n\\nThe reducer handles that action:\\n\\nTo make working with actions easier, action creators are often created — functions that return an action object:\\n\\nActions ensure a uniform way of describing events, which makes the code predictable and easy to test.\",\"const action = { type: 'INCREMENT' };\\n\\nconst addTodoAction = {\\n  type: 'ADD_TODO',\\n  payload: { text: 'Learn Redux', completed: false },\\n};\\n\\nstore.dispatch({ type: 'INCREMENT' });\\n\\nfunction counterReducer(state = 0, action) {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'INCREMENT':\\n      return state + 1;\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst increment = () => ({ type: 'INCREMENT' });\\nconst addTodo = (text) => ({\\n  type: 'ADD_TODO',\\n  payload: { text, completed: false },\\n});\\nstore.dispatch(increment());\\nstore.dispatch(addTodo('Learn Redux'));\"],[2832,\"The store in Redux manages the state of the whole application. It provides methods for obtaining the current state (`getState`), dispatching actions (`dispatch`) and subscribing to changes (`subscribe`). The store links the reducers with the actions in order to update the state.\",\"The store is an object that contains the state of the whole application and provides an API for working with that state. It is created by means of the `createStore` function and takes a reducer as its argument.\\nThe store's main methods:\\n\\n- `getState`:\\nReturns the application's current state.\\n\\n- `dispatch`:\\nUsed in order to dispatch actions. Once `dispatch` has been called, the reducers handle the action and return an updated state.\\n\\n- `subscribe`:\\nLets you subscribe to changes of state. Every time the state is updated, the function passed in is called.\\n\\nAn example of the complete use of the store:\\n\\nThe store ensures:\\n\\n- Centralized state: all of the data is held in one place.\\n\\n- Predictability: changes of state happen only by way of actions and reducers.\\n\\n- The ability to track: thanks to the `subscribe` method and to tools such as Redux DevTools.\\n\\nThe store's operation lets you manage state effectively, even in large applications with numerous components.\",\"const state = store.getState();\\nconsole.log(state);\\n\\nstore.dispatch({ type: 'INCREMENT' });\\n\\nconst unsubscribe = store.subscribe(() => {\\n  console.log('State updated:', store.getState());\\n});\\n// To cancel the subscription:\\nunsubscribe();\\n\\nimport { createStore } from 'redux';\\n// The reducer\\nfunction counterReducer(state = 0, action) {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'INCREMENT':\\n      return state + 1;\\n    case 'DECREMENT':\\n      return state - 1;\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n}\\n// Creating the store\\nconst store = createStore(counterReducer);\\n// Subscribing to the changes\\nstore.subscribe(() => {\\n  console.log('The current state:', store.getState());\\n});\\n// Dispatching the actions\\nstore.dispatch({ type: 'INCREMENT' });\\nstore.dispatch({ type: 'DECREMENT' });\"],[2833,\"Middleware in Redux are functions that wrap the `dispatch` method, adding additional behaviour to it. They are used for working with asynchronous operations, for logging, for error handling and for other tasks. Examples of popular middleware are `redux-thunk` and `redux-saga`.\",\"Middleware in Redux acts as a layer between the dispatching of an action (`dispatch`) and its handling in the reducer. They intercept every action, letting you perform additional logic before the action reaches the reducer. That is useful for:\\n\\n- Handling asynchronous operations (requests to an API, for example).\\n\\n- Logging the actions for debugging.\\n\\n- Handling side effects.\\n\\n- Validating data or modifying actions.\\n\\nAn example of standard middleware:\\n\\nConnecting middleware to the store is done through the `applyMiddleware` function:\\n\\nPopular examples of middleware:\\n\\n- `redux-thunk`: lets you dispatch functions rather than objects, for working with asynchronous code.\\n\\n- `redux-saga`: uses generators in order to manage complex flows of asynchronous operations.\\n\\n- Loggers: show the sequence of the actions and the changes of state.\\n\\nMiddleware makes Redux a powerful tool for working with real projects, adding flexibility and letting you handle complex scenarios easily.\",\"const loggerMiddleware = (store) => (next) => (action) => {\\n  console.log('Dispatching:', action);\\n  const result = next(action); // Passing the action further on\\n  console.log('Next state:', store.getState());\\n  return result;\\n};\\n\\nimport { createStore, applyMiddleware } from 'redux';\\n// A simple reducer\\nconst reducer = (state = {}, action) => state;\\n// Creating the store with the middleware\\nconst store = createStore(reducer, applyMiddleware(loggerMiddleware));\\n// An example of an action\\nstore.dispatch({ type: 'TEST_ACTION' });\"],[2834,\"`redux-thunk` is middleware that lets you dispatch functions rather than objects in Redux. Such functions can perform asynchronous code — requests to an API, for example — and then dispatch ordinary actions with the results of those operations. That is useful for managing state that depends on external data.\",\"By default Redux works only with synchronous actions, which are ordinary objects. In real applications, however, asynchronous operations such as requests to a server often have to be performed. `redux-thunk` is used for that.\\nHow does redux-thunk work?\\n\\n- `redux-thunk` intercepts the actions dispatched through `dispatch`.\\n\\n- If the action is a function, the middleware calls that function, passing `dispatch` and `getState` into it as arguments.\\n\\n- Inside that function you can perform asynchronous operations and dispatch ordinary actions.\\n\\nThe installation:\\n\\nThe connection:\\n\\nWe create the action function:\\n\\nWe dispatch that action:\\n\\nThe reducer handles ordinary actions:\\n\\nUsing `redux-thunk` lets you manage asynchronous processes effectively, making the code more organized and more predictable. That matters especially in applications where the state depends on external sources of data.\",\"npm install redux-thunk\\n\\nimport { createStore, applyMiddleware } from 'redux';\\nimport thunk from 'redux-thunk';\\n// A simple reducer\\nconst reducer = (state = { data: null }, action) => {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'SET_DATA':\\n      return { ...state, data: action.payload };\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n};\\n// The store with redux-thunk connected\\nconst store = createStore(reducer, applyMiddleware(thunk));\\n\\nconst fetchData = () => {\\n  return async (dispatch) => {\\n    dispatch({ type: 'FETCH_START' });\\n    try {\\n      const response = await fetch('https://api.example.com/data');\\n      const data = await response.json();\\n      dispatch({ type: 'SET_DATA', payload: data });\\n    } catch (error) {\\n      dispatch({ type: 'FETCH_ERROR', error });\\n    }\\n  };\\n};\\n\\nstore.dispatch(fetchData());\\n\\nconst reducer = (state = { loading: false, data: null, error: null }, action) => {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'FETCH_START':\\n      return { ...state, loading: true, error: null };\\n    case 'SET_DATA':\\n      return { ...state, loading: false, data: action.payload };\\n    case 'FETCH_ERROR':\\n      return { ...state, loading: false, error: action.error };\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n};\"],[2835,\"`redux-saga` is middleware for Redux that uses JavaScript generators in order to manage asynchronous actions. Instead of dispatching functions, as in `redux-thunk`, you describe the effects in \\\"sagas\\\", which watch for actions and perform the side effects (requests to an API, for example). That makes asynchronous code more structured and more predictable.\",\"`redux-saga` helps you handle side effects such as requests to an API, delays or interactions with external resources, without overfilling the logic in the components or the reducers. It is based on the concept of \\\"sagas\\\" — generator functions that respond to Redux's actions and perform the effects.\\nThe installation:\\n\\nCreating the reducer:\\n\\nCreating the saga: a saga is a generator function that watches for actions and performs the effects:\\n\\nConnecting the saga to the store:\\n\\nDispatching the actions:\\n\\nThe advantages of `redux-saga`:\\n\\n- The convenient organization of complex asynchronous operations.\\n\\n- The ability to test the generators.\\n\\n- Support for controlling the flows (`takeLatest`, for example, in order to perform only the latest action).\",\"npm install redux-saga\\n\\nconst initialState = { data: null, loading: false, error: null };\\nconst reducer = (state = initialState, action) => {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'FETCH_START':\\n      return { ...state, loading: true, error: null };\\n    case 'FETCH_SUCCESS':\\n      return { ...state, loading: false, data: action.payload };\\n    case 'FETCH_ERROR':\\n      return { ...state, loading: false, error: action.error };\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nimport { call, put, takeEvery } from 'redux-saga/effects';\\nfunction* fetchDataSaga() {\\n  try {\\n    yield put({ type: 'FETCH_START' }); // We notify that the loading has begun\\n    const data = yield call(() => fetch('https://api.example.com/data').then(res => res.json()));\\n    yield put({ type: 'FETCH_SUCCESS', payload: data }); // A successful result\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    yield put({ type: 'FETCH_ERROR', error }); // Handling the error\\n  }\\n}\\n// We watch for particular actions\\nfunction* watchFetchData() {\\n  yield takeEvery('FETCH_DATA', fetchDataSaga);\\n}\\n\\nimport createSagaMiddleware from 'redux-saga';\\nimport { createStore, applyMiddleware } from 'redux';\\nimport { all } from 'redux-saga/effects';\\nconst sagaMiddleware = createSagaMiddleware();\\n// The root saga\\nfunction* rootSaga() {\\n  yield all([watchFetchData()]); // Starting every \\\"watcher\\\"\\n}\\nconst store = createStore(reducer, applyMiddleware(sagaMiddleware));\\nsagaMiddleware.run(rootSaga); // Starting the sagas\\n\\nstore.dispatch({ type: 'FETCH_DATA' });\"],[2836,\"Selectors are functions that take Redux's state and return the data needed. They help you avoid repeating code and improve the readability. For complex computations on the state you can use libraries such as `reselect`, in order to memoize the results and raise the performance.\",\"Selectors in Redux are used in order to extract from the global state the data that is then passed to the components. Instead of accessing the state directly through `state.someProperty`, selectors provide an abstract, reusable way of obtaining the data.\\nA simple example of a selector:\\n\\nWhen the state becomes complex, selectors can combine data:\\n\\nFor optimization and in order to prevent repeated computations, the `reselect` library is used:\\nThe installation:\\n\\nCreating a memoized selector:\\n\\nThe advantages of using `reselect`:\\n\\n- Memoization: the selector's results are recomputed only when the input data changes.\\n\\n- Convenient work with nested states.\\n\\n- A rise in performance, especially in large applications.\\n\\nLibraries for working with selectors:\\n\\n- `reselect`: the most popular library for creating memoized selectors.\\n\\n- `re-reselect`: an extension of `reselect`, which lets you create selectors with caching based on parameters.\\n\\n- `redux-toolkit`: includes built-in support for memoized selectors through the `createSelector` API.\\n\\nSelectors ensure simple, performant and organized access to the data in Redux, especially in applications with a large volume of state.\",\"const selectTodos = (state) => state.todos;\\n\\n// Using it:\\nconst todos = selectTodos(store.getState());\\n\\nconst selectCompletedTodos = (state) =>\\n  state.todos.filter((todo) => todo.completed);\\n\\nconst completedTodos = selectCompletedTodos(store.getState());\\n\\nnpm install reselect\\n\\nimport { createSelector } from 'reselect';\\n// The primitive selectors\\nconst selectTodos = (state) => state.todos;\\nconst selectFilter = (state) => state.filter;\\n// The combined selector\\nconst selectFilteredTodos = createSelector(\\n  [selectTodos, selectFilter],\\n  (todos, filter) => {\\n    switch (filter) {\\n      case 'completed':\\n        return todos.filter((todo) => todo.completed);\\n      case 'active':\\n        return todos.filter((todo) => !todo.completed);\\n      default:\\n        return todos;\\n    }\\n  }\\n);\\nconst filteredTodos = selectFilteredTodos(store.getState());\"],[2837,\"`Reselect` is a library for creating selectors, which extract and process data from the Redux state. It provides memoization, which prevents unnecessary computations if the input data has not changed. That is especially useful for complex computations that depend on the state.\",\"`Reselect` is a library that makes creating selectors for working with Redux easier. Selectors are functions that extract data from the state. Simple selectors return the data directly, but for more complex computations several parts of the state may have to be combined. In such cases, without memoization the same computations will be repeated every time the selector is called, even if the data has not changed.\\nMemoization is `reselect`'s key feature. It saves the selector's result and recomputes it only when the input data has changed. That raises the application's performance.\\nAn example of using `reselect`:\\nThe installation:\\n\\nCreating the selectors:\\n\\nThe advantages:\\n\\n- Optimization: avoids performing complex computations again.\\n\\n- Organization: improves the readability of the code by abstracting the data-access logic away.\\n\\n- Reuse: one selector can be used in several components.\\n\\n`Reselect` is often used in large applications where there are complex computations on the state. That lets you optimize the performance and make managing the state easier.\",\"npm install reselect\\n\\nimport { createSelector } from 'reselect';\\n// The primitive selectors\\nconst selectTodos = (state) => state.todos;\\nconst selectFilter = (state) => state.filter;\\n// The memoized selector\\nconst selectFilteredTodos = createSelector(\\n  [selectTodos, selectFilter],\\n  (todos, filter) => {\\n    switch (filter) {\\n      case 'completed':\\n        return todos.filter((todo) => todo.completed);\\n      case 'active':\\n        return todos.filter((todo) => !todo.completed);\\n      default:\\n        return todos;\\n    }\\n  }\\n);\\n// Using it:\\nconst filteredTodos = selectFilteredTodos(store.getState());\"],[2838,\"`combineReducers` is a Redux function that combines several reducers into one. That is convenient when an application's state is divided into logical parts (users, tasks, filters, for example). Each reducer manages its own part of the state, and `combineReducers` combines them into a single structure.\",\"When an application becomes complex, dividing the state into separate parts (or slices) helps you organize the code. Each slice can be managed by its own reducer. The `combineReducers` function lets you combine those reducers into a single root reducer, which is passed into the Redux store.\\nAn example of its use:\\n\\n- Separate reducers for each slice of the state:\\n\\n- Combining the reducers:\\n\\n- Creating the store:\\n\\n- An example of the state: once `combineReducers` has been used, the state will look like this:\\n\\n- Accessing the data: the components can extract the data from the corresponding parts of the state:\\n\\nWhat is combineReducers for?\\n\\n- Simplifying the structure: dividing the logic into separate reducers makes the code more readable and more maintainable.\\n\\n- Scalability: it is easier to add new reducers as the application grows.\\n\\n- Flexibility: it lets every reducer focus only on its own part of the state.\\n\\nIn large applications the use of `combineReducers` is becoming the standard, since it helps you maintain order in the code and make working with the state easier.\",\"const todosReducer = (state = [], action) => {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'ADD_TODO':\\n      return [...state, action.payload];\\n    case 'REMOVE_TODO':\\n      return state.filter((todo) => todo.id !== action.payload);\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n};\\nconst filterReducer = (state = 'all', action) => {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'SET_FILTER':\\n      return action.payload;\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nimport { combineReducers } from 'redux';\\nconst rootReducer = combineReducers({\\n  todos: todosReducer,\\n  filter: filterReducer,\\n});\\nexport default rootReducer;\\n\\nimport { createStore } from 'redux';\\nimport rootReducer from './reducers';\\nconst store = createStore(rootReducer);\\n\\n{\\n  todos: [{ id: 1, text: 'Learn Redux' }],\\n  filter: 'all',\\n}\\n\\nconst todos = store.getState().todos;\\nconst filter = store.getState().filter;\"],[2839,\"To connect a component to the Redux store, the React Redux library is used. The `useSelector` hook is applied in order to obtain data from the state, and `useDispatch` in order to dispatch actions. These tools let you integrate Redux logic into React components without the need for additional configuration.\",\"The linking of a React component with the Redux store is carried out through the React Redux library, which provides the `useSelector` and `useDispatch` hooks.\\nThe steps for connecting it:\\nMake sure the Redux store has been passed into the application through the `<Provider>` component:\\n\\nUse `useSelector` in order to access the state:\\n\\nUse `useDispatch` in order to dispatch actions:\\n\\nThe key aspects:\\n\\n- `useSelector`: lets you \\\"subscribe\\\" to changes in the Redux state and obtain its data.\\n\\n- `useDispatch`: provides the function for dispatching actions, in order to change the state.\\n\\nAn alternative approach — connect (the classic API): in older versions of React Redux the `connect` function was used:\\n\\n`useSelector` and `useDispatch` are the more modern and more convenient way of working with Redux in functional components.\",\"import { Provider } from 'react-redux';\\nimport { store } from './store';\\nconst App = () => (\\n  <Provider store={store}>\\n    <YourComponent />\\n  </Provider>\\n);\\n\\nimport { useSelector } from 'react-redux';\\nconst YourComponent = () => {\\n  const todos = useSelector((state) => state.todos);\\n  return (\\n    <ul>\\n      {todos.map((todo) => (\\n        <li key={todo.id}>{todo.text}</li>\\n      ))}\\n    </ul>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\nimport { useDispatch } from 'react-redux';\\nconst YourComponent = () => {\\n  const dispatch = useDispatch();\\n  const addTodo = () => {\\n    dispatch({ type: 'ADD_TODO', payload: { text: 'New Task' } });\\n  };\\n  return <button onClick={addTodo}>Add Todo</button>;\\n};\\n\\nimport { connect } from 'react-redux';\\nconst mapStateToProps = (state) => ({\\n  todos: state.todos,\\n});\\nconst mapDispatchToProps = (dispatch) => ({\\n  addTodo: () => dispatch({ type: 'ADD_TODO', payload: { text: 'New Task' } }),\\n});\\nconst YourComponent = ({ todos, addTodo }) => (\\n  <div>\\n    <ul>\\n      {todos.map((todo) => (\\n        <li key={todo.id}>{todo.text}</li>\\n      ))}\\n    </ul>\\n    <button onClick={addTodo}>Add Todo</button>\\n  </div>\\n);\\nexport default connect(mapStateToProps, mapDispatchToProps)(YourComponent);\"],[2840,\"Redux Toolkit is the official library from Redux's developers, which makes configuring the store and creating reducers and actions easier. It eliminates repetitive code and provides tools for working with asynchronous operations. It is the recommended way of using Redux in modern applications.\",\"Redux Toolkit (RTK) is a superstructure over Redux, designed to make working with it simpler and more standardized. In traditional Redux, developers often run into a great deal of boilerplate code: the manual creation of actions and reducers, the configuration of middleware and so on. Redux Toolkit solves those problems by providing a set of utilities for creating an application quickly and effectively.\\nThe key features of Redux Toolkit:\\n\\n- `configureStore`: makes creating the store easier and connects useful middleware automatically (for working with asynchronicity, for example).\\n\\n- `createSlice`: lets you combine a reducer and the actions in one place, eliminating the need to define the action types by hand.\\n\\n- `createAsyncThunk`: makes working with asynchronous actions easier, handling their state (loading, success, error) automatically.\\n\\nMemoization and convenient tools: integration with debugging tools such as Redux DevTools.\\nAn example of using Redux Toolkit:\\n\\nAsynchronous actions with `createAsyncThunk`:\\n\\nWhy is Redux Toolkit useful?\\n\\n- Less boilerplate code: you write only what matters for the business logic.\\n\\n- Convenience: tools such as `createSlice` handle the creation of the actions and the reducers automatically.\\n\\n- Safety: it includes the best practices by default — the use of `Immer` for working with immutable states, for example.\\n\\n- Integration: it works easily with React, Redux DevTools and middleware.\\n\\nRedux Toolkit has become the standard for development on Redux, and it is recommended for use in all new projects.\",\"import { configureStore, createSlice } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';\\n// Creating a slice\\nconst todosSlice = createSlice({\\n  name: 'todos',\\n  initialState: [],\\n  reducers: {\\n    addTodo: (state, action) => {\\n      state.push(action.payload);\\n    },\\n    removeTodo: (state, action) => {\\n      return state.filter((todo) => todo.id !== action.payload);\\n    },\\n  },\\n});\\n// Exporting the actions\\nexport const { addTodo, removeTodo } = todosSlice.actions;\\n// Creating the store\\nconst store = configureStore({\\n  reducer: {\\n    todos: todosSlice.reducer,\\n  },\\n});\\nexport default store;\\n\\nimport { createAsyncThunk } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';\\nexport const fetchTodos = createAsyncThunk('todos/fetchTodos', async () => {\\n  const response = await fetch('/api/todos');\\n  return response.json();\\n});\"],[2841,\"`createSlice` is a utility from Redux Toolkit that lets you combine reducers and actions in a single object. That makes creating reducers easier, removes the need to write the action types by hand and improves the readability of the code. Action creators and reducers are generated automatically for those actions.\",\"`createSlice` is a function from Redux Toolkit that makes creating reducers and actions for working with state easier. Normally in Redux you had to create the actions (the action types) and the reducers by hand, which led to a great deal of boilerplate code. `createSlice` eliminates that problem by letting you describe the reducers and the actions in one place.\\nHow to use `createSlice`:\\nInto `createSlice` you pass an object with several keys:\\n\\n- `name`: the name of the slice of state, which will be used in order to generate the prefix of the action types.\\n\\n- `initialState`: the initial state.\\n\\n- `reducers`: an object containing the reducer functions, which update the state.\\n\\nAn example of using `createSlice`:\\n\\nThe advantages of `createSlice`:\\n\\n- The simplification of the code: `createSlice` combines the creation of the reducers and the actions in one place.\\n\\n- The automatic creation of the actions: for every reducer the corresponding action is created automatically (`addTodo`, for example).\\n\\n- Readability and maintainability: less repetitive code and a better structure.\\n\\n- Built-in support for immutability: the Immer library is used, which lets you change the state \\\"mutably\\\" while it in fact remains unchanged.\\n\\nAn example of its use:\\n\\nSo `createSlice` makes development considerably easier, improves the readability of the code and speeds the development process up, removing the need to write out the action types and the reducers by hand.\",\"import { createSlice } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';\\nconst todosSlice = createSlice({\\n  name: 'todos', // The name of the slice of state\\n  initialState: [], // The initial state (an empty list of tasks)\\n  reducers: {\\n    addTodo: (state, action) => {\\n      state.push(action.payload); // Adding a task\\n    },\\n    removeTodo: (state, action) => {\\n      return state.filter(todo => todo.id !== action.payload); // Removing a task\\n    }\\n  }\\n});\\n// The automatically generated actions\\nexport const { addTodo, removeTodo } = todosSlice.actions;\\n// The reducer\\nexport default todosSlice.reducer;\\n\\nimport { configureStore } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';\\nimport todosReducer from './todosSlice';\\nconst store = configureStore({\\n  reducer: {\\n    todos: todosReducer\\n  }\\n});\"],[2842,\"`createReducer` is a function from Redux Toolkit that helps you create reducers without having to define the action types by hand. It takes an object with the state and the actions, and each reducer updates the state depending on the type of the action. That lets you write reducers more compactly and more effectively.\",\"`createReducer` is a function provided by Redux Toolkit, which lets you create reducers without specifying the action types explicitly. Instead of the traditional approach, with the manual creation of an action type and the handling of each of them in the reducer, `createReducer` lets you bind the actions to the change of state straight away by means of an object in which the keys are the action types and the values are the reducers.\\nHow `createReducer` works:\\n`createReducer` takes two arguments:\\n\\n- The initial state.\\n\\n- An object in which the keys are the action types and the values are functions that change the state.\\n\\n- For every action type the reducer will call the corresponding function.\\n\\nAn example of using `createReducer`:\\n\\nThe advantages of `createReducer`:\\n\\n- The cleanliness of the code: the reducers look more compact, since there is no need to specify a `switch` and the action types explicitly.\\n\\n- Automatic immutability: inside the reducer the Immer library is applied, which lets you change the state \\\"mutably\\\" while it in fact remains unchanged.\\n\\n- Flexibility: you can add any actions and change the state in a single function, which makes maintenance easier.\\n\\nHow to use it in the store:\\n\\n`createReducer` is ideal for the cases where a reducer has to be created without specifying the action types explicitly and you want to get rid of boilerplate code. That makes the code simpler, cleaner and easier to maintain, especially for large projects.\",\"import { createReducer } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';\\nconst initialState = [];\\nconst todosReducer = createReducer(initialState, {\\n  ADD_TODO: (state, action) => {\\n    state.push(action.payload); // Adding a task\\n  },\\n  REMOVE_TODO: (state, action) => {\\n    return state.filter(todo => todo.id !== action.payload); // Removing a task\\n  }\\n});\\nexport default todosReducer;\\n\\nimport { configureStore } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';\\nimport todosReducer from './todosReducer';\\nconst store = configureStore({\\n  reducer: {\\n    todos: todosReducer\\n  }\\n});\"],[2843,\"`createAsyncThunk` is a utility from Redux Toolkit for working with asynchronous actions. It helps you create asynchronous actions, generating three states automatically: pending, success and error. You describe the asynchronous function, and Redux Toolkit manages the loading and error state automatically, updating the store on the basis of the result of the action.\",\"`createAsyncThunk` is a function from Redux Toolkit intended to make working with asynchronous actions in Redux easier. It creates three actions automatically for every asynchronous request: one for the start of the request, one for its successful completion and one for an error. Those actions help you keep the loading and error state in the application under control without requiring additional code.\\nHow to use `createAsyncThunk`:\\n\\n- The first argument is a string, which defines the type of the action.\\n\\n- The second argument is an asynchronous function, which returns a promise or performs an asynchronous operation — a request to an API, for example.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nHow it works with asynchronous state: in the course of `createAsyncThunk`'s operation, three actions are created automatically:\\n\\n- pending — when the request has been sent but has not finished yet.\\n\\n- fulfilled — when the request has finished successfully.\\n\\n- rejected — when the request has finished with an error.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe advantages of `createAsyncThunk`:\\n\\n- It manages the loading and error state automatically.\\n\\n- It reduces the amount of code that has to be written by hand in order to handle asynchronous operations.\\n\\n- It makes managing asynchronous requests easier, improving the readability and the maintainability of the code.\",\"import { createAsyncThunk } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';\\nconst fetchData = createAsyncThunk('data/fetchData', async () => {\\n  const response = await fetch('/api/data');\\n  return response.json(); // returns the data\\n});\\n\\nimport { createSlice } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';\\nconst dataSlice = createSlice({\\n  name: 'data',\\n  initialState: { data: [], loading: false, error: null },\\n  reducers: {},\\n  extraReducers: (builder) => {\\n    builder\\n      .addCase(fetchData.pending, (state) => {\\n        state.loading = true;\\n        state.error = null;\\n      })\\n      .addCase(fetchData.fulfilled, (state, action) => {\\n        state.data = action.payload;\\n        state.loading = false;\\n      })\\n      .addCase(fetchData.rejected, (state, action) => {\\n        state.loading = false;\\n        state.error = action.error.message;\\n      });\\n  }\\n});\"],[2844,\"RTK (Redux Toolkit) makes working with asynchronous requests and side effects easier by means of `createAsyncThunk`, which manages the state of the requests automatically. It creates three states for every asynchronous action (pending, success, error) and updates the store depending on the result. RTK also makes the handling of side effects such as API calls easier, through convenient mechanisms for working with asynchronous data.\",\"RTK provides tools for working with asynchronous requests and side effects through functions such as `createAsyncThunk` and the built-in capabilities for handling those effects in `extraReducers`. Unlike ordinary Redux, where the state and the action types had to be managed by hand in order to work with asynchronous actions, RTK offers a simpler and more convenient way.\\nThe main mechanisms:\\n\\n- `createAsyncThunk`: this is a convenient way of handling asynchronous requests. It generates three actions automatically (pending, fulfilled, rejected) for working with the loading, data and error state. That simplifies the code and eliminates the need to track the progress of the requests by hand.\\n\\nHow RTK manages side effects: when asynchronous operations such as requests to a server have to be performed, RTK lets you handle them by means of `createAsyncThunk`. In response to the successful completion of a request (fulfilled), the data is updated in the state automatically. On an error (rejected), the information about the error is saved in the state.\\nAn example:\\n\\nHandling side effects in `extraReducers`: in RTK, side effects can be handled in `extraReducers`, where you describe how the state changes depending on each type of asynchronous action.\\n\\nThe advantages of RTK for asynchronous requests:\\n\\n- The simplification of the code: less need to handle the loading and error state by hand.\\n\\n- The automatic creation of the actions for performing asynchronous operations.\\n\\n- Support for working with asynchronous operations through `createAsyncThunk`, which considerably improves the maintainability and the readability of the code.\\n\\nSo RTK helps developers save time, improve the quality of their code and work easily with asynchronous requests and side effects in Redux.\",\"const fetchData = createAsyncThunk('data/fetchData', async () => {\\n  const response = await fetch('/api/data');\\n  return response.json();\\n});\\n\\nconst dataSlice = createSlice({\\n  name: 'data',\\n  initialState: { data: [], loading: false, error: null },\\n  reducers: {},\\n  extraReducers: (builder) => {\\n    builder\\n      .addCase(fetchData.pending, (state) => {\\n        state.loading = true;\\n        state.error = null;\\n      })\\n      .addCase(fetchData.fulfilled, (state, action) => {\\n        state.data = action.payload;\\n        state.loading = false;\\n      })\\n      .addCase(fetchData.rejected, (state, action) => {\\n        state.loading = false;\\n        state.error = action.error.message;\\n      });\\n  }\\n});\"],[2845,\"`createEntityAdapter` is a Redux Toolkit utility intended to make working with collections of objects easier. It provides a set of methods for adding, removing, updating and sorting the objects in an array. That lets you manage the state more effectively, especially when you have to work with a large number of objects, such as records from a database.\",\"`createEntityAdapter` provides a set of tools for working with collections of data in Redux. This tool helps you manage state that contains a multitude of objects, making it easier to add, update and remove them without writing a great deal of code.\\nHow `createEntityAdapter` works:\\nIt provides methods for working with collections — `addOne`, `removeOne`, `updateOne`, for example — which make updating the state easier.\\nInstead of holding an array of objects, you can use a more effective structure with a key and the objects, which speeds the search and update operations up.\\nAn example of its use:\\n\\nThe advantages:\\n\\n- It lets you work with large lists of data easily.\\n\\n- It uses a more optimized structure for holding the data (keys and values), which makes the search and update operations easier.\",\"import { createEntityAdapter, createSlice } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';\\n// We create the adapter\\nconst usersAdapter = createEntityAdapter();\\n// Initializing the state\\nconst initialState = usersAdapter.getInitialState({\\n  loading: false,\\n  error: null\\n});\\n// We create the slice\\nconst usersSlice = createSlice({\\n  name: 'users',\\n  initialState,\\n  reducers: {\\n    setUsers: usersAdapter.setAll,\\n    addUser: usersAdapter.addOne,\\n  }\\n});\\nexport const { setUsers, addUser } = usersSlice.actions;\\nexport default usersSlice.reducer;\"],[2846,\"`createSelector` from the `reselect` library lets you create memoized selectors, which extract and compute data from the Redux state effectively. With RTK it is used in order to create performant selectors, which avoid unnecessary recomputations if the data has not changed.\",\"`createSelector` is a function from the `reselect` library, which helps you create memoized selectors. That means the selectors will be computed only when the data they use changes, which raises the application's performance.\\nHow to use it with RTK:\\nTo create a selector using `createSelector`, you have to pass in the functions that extract the data from the Redux state, and then carry out additional computations if necessary.\\nAn example of its use with RTK:\\n\\nThe advantages:\\n\\n- Selectors with memoization let you avoid superfluous recomputations and improve the application's performance.\\n\\n- You can combine selectors for more complex computations while remaining effective.\",\"import { createSelector } from 'reselect';\\n// A selector for obtaining every user\\nconst selectUsers = (state) => state.users.entities;\\n// A memoized selector for obtaining the active users\\nconst selectActiveUsers = createSelector(\\n  [selectUsers],\\n  (users) => users.filter(user => user.active)\\n);\"],[2847,\"RTK Query is a powerful tool from Redux Toolkit, intended to make working with API requests and the caching of data easier. It generates the actions, reducers and requests for interacting with a REST API or other sources of data automatically. RTK Query lets you handle the loading, successful-response and error states automatically.\",\"RTK Query is a tool for working with server-side requests, which automates many processes, such as the creation of actions, the handling of the loading and error states, and the management of the caching of data.\\nHow RTK Query works:\\n\\n- In RTK Query a service is created with a description of the API's endpoints, and for every request the actions and reducers are generated automatically.\\n\\n- It manages the state of the requests, including the loading and error states, and can cache the responses in order to prevent repeated requests.\\n\\nAn example of its use:\\n\\nThe advantages:\\n\\n- The automatic generation of the actions and the reducers.\\n\\n- The handling of the requests' states without having to manage every request by hand.\\n\\n- Built-in caching and the optimization of the requests, which reduces the load on the server and improves the application's performance.\",\"import { createApi, fetchBaseQuery } from '@reduxjs/toolkit/query/react';\\n// Creating the API with the endpoints\\nconst api = createApi({\\n  reducerPath: 'api',\\n  baseQuery: fetchBaseQuery({ baseUrl: '/api/' }),\\n  endpoints: (builder) => ({\\n    getUsers: builder.query({\\n      query: () => 'users',\\n    }),\\n  }),\\n});\\n// Using the hook in a component\\nconst { data, error, isLoading } = useGetUsersQuery();\"],[2848,\"RTK Query helps you integrate API requests into an application by means of the automatic generation of actions and reducers. You create a service with the endpoints and then use the generated hooks in the components in order to perform the requests and handle the loading and error states.\",\"RTK Query makes working with API requests easier by automating many processes, such as the creation of actions for the requests, the handling of the states and the caching of the responses.\\nHow to use RTK Query for API requests:\\n\\n- First you create a service with the endpoints, describing the routes and the requests.\\n\\n- Then you use the generated hooks in the components in order to perform the requests and work with the state.\\n\\nAn example of its use:\\n\\nThe advantages of using RTK Query:\\n\\n- The automatic creation of actions for every request.\\n\\n- The management of the loading and error states with minimal effort.\\n\\n- The caching of the requests, which improves the performance.\",\"import { createApi, fetchBaseQuery } from '@reduxjs/toolkit/query/react';\\nconst api = createApi({\\n  reducerPath: 'api',\\n  baseQuery: fetchBaseQuery({ baseUrl: '/api/' }),\\n  endpoints: (builder) => ({\\n    getPosts: builder.query({\\n      query: () => 'posts',\\n    }),\\n  }),\\n});\\n// The hook for obtaining the data\\nconst { data, error, isLoading } = useGetPostsQuery();\"],[2849,\"To optimize a Redux application you can use several techniques. First, it is worth minimizing the number of `dispatch` calls and state updates. Second, you should avoid superfluous renders of the components, using memoization, `reselect` or `React.memo`. It is also useful to normalize the data in the store, in order to make extracting and managing it easier. The use of middleware such as `redux-thunk` will help you manage asynchronous operations more effectively.\",\"To improve the performance of a Redux application it matters to optimize the rendering of the components and the management of the state. Here are several key approaches:\\n\\n- Minimizing the changes of state: try to update only the parts of the state that really do change. Avoid global changes of state, since that can cause a re-render of every component connected to Redux.\\n\\n- Memoization by means of `reselect`: to optimize computations that may be expensive, use memoization. `Reselect` helps you cache the selectors' results, preventing repeated computations if the input data has not changed.\\n\\n- The use of `React.memo` and `useMemo`: these React hooks let you avoid unnecessary renders. If a component has not changed, `React.memo` lets you avoid rendering it again.\\n\\n- The normalization of the data: hold the data in a format that makes access easier and minimizes the need to update the whole state. Instead of an array of objects, for example, use objects with unique identifiers, so as to extract and change the data more simply.\\n\\n- Lazy loading and code splitting: load only the parts of the application that are needed, so as to speed the initial load up and reduce the load on the application.\\n\\nAn example of the normalization of data:\",\"// We normalize the array of data:\\nconst initialState = {\\n  users: {\\n    1: { id: 1, name: 'Alice' },\\n    2: { id: 2, name: 'Bob' }\\n  }\\n};\\n// Instead of this:\\nconst initialState = {\\n  users: [\\n    { id: 1, name: 'Alice' },\\n    { id: 2, name: 'Bob' }\\n  ]\\n};\"],[2850,\"Memoization is an optimization technique in which a function's results are saved and used again if the input data has not changed. In Redux, memoization is used by means of the `reselect` library, which lets you cache the selectors' results and avoid computing them again. That is especially useful when working with large sets of data, where the computations may be expensive.\",\"Memoization lets you avoid computing a function again with the same arguments, which speeds the application's operation up considerably. In Redux, memoization is often used in combination with the `reselect` library in order to improve the performance when extracting data from the store.\\nWhen we create a selector by means of `reselect`, it remembers the results of the function for particular input data. If the data passed into the selector has not changed, the selector returns the result that has already been computed, which saves resources and prevents repeated computations.\\nAn example of using memoization with `reselect`:\\n\\nIn this example the `getFilteredUsers` selector will cache the result of the filtering of the users. If `users` or `filter` does not change, the previous result will be returned without a recomputation.\\nSo memoization helps you reduce the number of computations, improving the performance, especially in applications with large volumes of data.\",\"import { createSelector } from 'reselect';\\n// The selectors for extracting the data\\nconst getUsers = state => state.users;\\nconst getUserFilter = state => state.filter;\\n// The memoized selector\\nconst getFilteredUsers = createSelector(\\n  [getUsers, getUserFilter],\\n  (users, filter) => {\\n    return users.filter(user => user.name.includes(filter));\\n  }\\n);\"],[2851,\"The normalization of data in Redux is the process of converting complex, nested data structures into a flatter form, in which every value is held under a unique identifier. It matters because it makes extracting the data easier, minimizes duplication and lets you update the data more effectively. Instead of holding an array of objects with the same properties, for example, you can use an object in which the keys are the unique identifiers.\",\"The normalization of data in Redux consists in simplifying the structure of the store, so as to avoid redundant data and make access to it easier. When the data is not normalized — when an array of objects with the same properties is repeated, for example — that leads to redundancy and to the complexity of updates. Normalization solves that problem by representing the data in the form of a flat structure, in which every element can be found by a unique key.\\nAn example of non-normalized data:\\n\\nIn that case, if the address changes for several users, it will have to be updated in every object, which leads to the duplication of data and increases the likelihood of errors.\\nAn example of normalized data:\\n\\nIn this case we hold only the unique data for every user and address, which makes updates easier. If the city has to be changed, we simply update the record in `addresses`, without touching the array of users.\",\"const state = {\\n  users: [\\n    { id: 1, name: 'Alice', address: { city: 'NY' } },\\n    { id: 2, name: 'Bob', address: { city: 'LA' } },\\n  ]\\n};\\n\\nconst state = {\\n  users: {\\n    1: { id: 1, name: 'Alice', addressId: 101 },\\n    2: { id: 2, name: 'Bob', addressId: 102 }\\n  },\\n  addresses: {\\n    101: { city: 'NY' },\\n    102: { city: 'LA' }\\n  }\\n};\"],[2852,\"To manage state effectively in large applications with Redux, it matters to divide the state into smaller parts and to use middleware for the asynchronous operations. The application of `combineReducers` helps you organize the logic, and the use of the normalization of data makes the store more compact and makes access to the data easier. It is also worth optimizing the performance by means of memoization, selectors and memoized hooks such as `React.memo`.\",\"When an application grows and the state becomes complex, it matters to have strategies for managing that state effectively. Here are several recommendations for working with a large application using Redux:\\n\\n- Dividing the state: use `combineReducers` in order to organize the state into smaller, independent parts. That lets you maintain a more structured and more scalable store. The logic can be divided into reducers for the users, the products, the orders and so on, for example.\\n\\n- The normalization of the data: when an application manages a large volume of data, normalization becomes a key factor. Instead of holding arrays of objects with duplicated data, for example, it is better to hold the data under unique identifiers. That lets you update and obtain the data easily without superfluous load.\\n\\n- Asynchronous operations: in large applications you often have to work with asynchronous requests. For that it is better to use middleware such as `redux-thunk`, `redux-saga` or `RTK Query`, in order to manage the side effects and the requests to the server effectively. That helps you avoid cluttering the component up and makes handling the asynchronous operations easier.\\n\\n- Optimizing the performance: use memoization in order to prevent superfluous renders. For that you can apply selectors from `reselect`, which cache the results of the computations. Also apply `React.memo` to the components, so as to avoid unnecessary renders.\\n\\n- The use of Redux Toolkit (RTK): RTK makes configuring Redux in applications easier, cutting the amount of boilerplate code down and providing useful functions such as `createSlice` and `createAsyncThunk`, which help you simplify the work with the state and with asynchronous actions.\\n\\nAn example of using `combineReducers`:\\n\\nAn example of using `createSlice`:\\n\\nSo to manage the state of a large application it matters to maintain the structure of the data, optimize the rendering, use asynchronous operations and organize the logic properly by means of Redux Toolkit's tools.\",\"import { combineReducers } from 'redux';\\nconst rootReducer = combineReducers({\\n  users: usersReducer,\\n  posts: postsReducer,\\n  comments: commentsReducer\\n});\\n\\nimport { createSlice } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';\\nconst userSlice = createSlice({\\n  name: 'users',\\n  initialState: [],\\n  reducers: {\\n    setUsers: (state, action) => {\\n      return action.payload;\\n    }\\n  }\\n});\\nexport const { setUsers } = userSlice.actions;\\nexport default userSlice.reducer;\"],[2853,\"To handle authorization and authentication using Redux, the state is normally held in a reducer, which holds data such as the access token, the information about the user and a flag indicating the authorization status. After a successful login the token can be saved in the Redux store, and on a logout removed. Middleware is also often used for asynchronous requests, such as authorization through an API.\",\"The handling of authorization and authentication in an application using Redux normally includes several stages:\\n\\n- Holding the data about the user in Redux: when the user logs in, data such as the authorization token or the information about the user is saved in Redux. That lets you manage the state centrally and access that data in any of the application's components.\\n\\n- The use of middleware for asynchronous actions: asynchronous operations, such as requests to the server for authorization, are normally handled by means of middleware — `redux-thunk` or `redux-saga`, for example. Those tools let you send the requests and handle a successful or an unsuccessful response, updating the authorization state in Redux.\\n\\nAn example of a reducer for authentication:\\n\\nSaving the tokens and safety: it matters to remember that for safety, tokens and other sensitive data must not be held in the Redux store in the open. It is better to use other ways of holding them, such as `localStorage` or `sessionStorage`, and the data from the Redux store can be synchronized with those stores.\\nAn example of authorization using middleware:\",\"const initialState = {\\n  isAuthenticated: false,\\n  user: null,\\n  token: null,\\n  loading: false,\\n  error: null\\n};\\nconst authReducer = (state = initialState, action) => {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'LOGIN_REQUEST':\\n      return { ...state, loading: true };\\n    case 'LOGIN_SUCCESS':\\n      return { ...state, loading: false, isAuthenticated: true, user: action.payload.user, token: action.payload.token };\\n    case 'LOGIN_FAILURE':\\n      return { ...state, loading: false, error: action.payload.error };\\n    case 'LOGOUT':\\n      return { ...state, isAuthenticated: false, user: null, token: null };\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n};\\n\\n// An example with redux-thunk for asynchronous authorization\\nconst loginUser = (credentials) => async (dispatch) => {\\n  try {\\n    dispatch({ type: 'LOGIN_REQUEST' });\\n    const response = await api.login(credentials);\\n    dispatch({ type: 'LOGIN_SUCCESS', payload: { user: response.data, token: response.token } });\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    dispatch({ type: 'LOGIN_FAILURE', payload: { error: error.message } });\\n  }\\n};\"],[2854,\"To manage the state of multi-component forms by means of Redux, the form's data can be held in the global state. Every component of the form can be linked with part of that state through `mapStateToProps` and `mapDispatchToProps`. It is also useful to use events, such as the changing of the form's fields, in order to update the state through actions in Redux.\",\"Multi-component forms can be complex to manage, especially if every component handles part of the form's data. In that case Redux provides a convenient solution for holding and managing the form's state centrally.\\n\\n- The structure of the form's state: a structure for holding all of the form's values is created in the reducer. That lets you update and obtain the form's state centrally, and ensure consistency between the components.\\n\\n- The form's components: every component of the form can be linked with part of the form's state through `connect` or through the `useSelector` and `useDispatch` hooks. When the user enters data, the components dispatch actions in order to update the corresponding fields of the state.\\n\\nAn example of a reducer for a form:\\n\\nThe form's components:\\n\\nManaging the submission of the form: once every field of the form has been filled in, the action for submitting the data can be linked with Redux. The form's data will be sent through actions, which are handled in the reducer.\\nAn example of an action for submitting the form's data:\\n\\nUsing Redux to manage multi-component forms helps you track the state of every field centrally, making the synchronization of the data easier and preventing the duplication of logic in the components.\",\"const initialState = {\\n  name: '',\\n  email: '',\\n  password: '',\\n};\\nconst formReducer = (state = initialState, action) => {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'SET_FORM_FIELD':\\n      return { ...state, [action.payload.field]: action.payload.value };\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nconst NameField = () => {\\n  const dispatch = useDispatch();\\n  const name = useSelector(state => state.form.name);\\n  const handleChange = (e) => {\\n    dispatch({ type: 'SET_FORM_FIELD', payload: { field: 'name', value: e.target.value } });\\n  };\\n  return <input type=\\\"text\\\" value={name} onChange={handleChange} />;\\n};\\nconst EmailField = () => {\\n  const dispatch = useDispatch();\\n  const email = useSelector(state => state.form.email);\\n  const handleChange = (e) => {\\n    dispatch({ type: 'SET_FORM_FIELD', payload: { field: 'email', value: e.target.value } });\\n  };\\n  return <input type=\\\"email\\\" value={email} onChange={handleChange} />;\\n};\\n\\nconst submitForm = (formData) => async (dispatch) => {\\n  try {\\n    await api.submitForm(formData);\\n    dispatch({ type: 'FORM_SUBMIT_SUCCESS' });\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    dispatch({ type: 'FORM_SUBMIT_FAILURE', payload: error });\\n  }\\n};\"],[2855,\"`useReducer` is a React hook that manages complex state by way of a reducer (a function that handles actions). It is worth using:\\n\\n- When the state is complex (many related values — a form with validation, for example).\\n\\n- When the update logic is non-trivial (many conditions, side effects).\\n\\n- To optimize the performance (reducers help you avoid superfluous re-renders).\\n\\n- As a simplified alternative to Redux (if global state is not needed).\",\"1. How does `useReducer` work?\\n`useReducer` takes three arguments:\\n\\n- The reducer — a function of the form `(state, action) => newState`, which determines how the state is to change in response to an action.\\n\\n- The initial state.\\n\\n- (Optionally) a function for the lazy initialization of the state.\\n\\nIt returns an array of two elements:\\n\\n- The current state.\\n\\n- The `dispatch` function, which sends actions into the reducer.\\n\\nA simple example of a counter:\\n\\nWhen do you use `useReducer` instead of `useState`?\\n`useState` suits simple state well — for holding a single value or a primitive data structure, for example. `useReducer` nonetheless becomes the preferable choice in the following cases:\\n\\n- The state has a complex structure. For example, if you have to manage a form with a multitude of fields, validation and dependent values.\\n\\n- The logic for updating the state is complex. If the changes of state depend on the previous state or require a multitude of conditions to be handled, a reducer helps you organize the code better.\\n\\n- The re-renders have to be minimized. `useReducer` lets you group several changes of state into one update, which can improve the performance.\\n\\nAn example of a complex form with `useReducer`:\\n\\nWhen do you use `useReducer` instead of Redux?\\nRedux is a tool for managing an application's global state, but it is not always needed. `useReducer` can be an excellent alternative in the following cases:\\n\\n- The state is local. If the state is needed only inside one component or a small group of components, `useReducer` will be simpler and more convenient.\\n\\n- Redux's additional capabilities are not needed. For example, if you do not require middleware (such as Redux Thunk or Saga), DevTools or time-travel debugging.\\n\\n- You want to avoid superfluous complexity. Redux requires the configuration of a store, actions and reducers, which can be excessive for small projects.\\n\\nWhen to choose Redux:\\n\\n- Global state. If the state has to be available in many parts of the application.\\n\\n- Complex side effects. Asynchronous requests handled through middleware, for example.\\n\\n- Developer tools. Redux DevTools provide powerful capabilities for debugging.\\n\\nIn summary:\\n\\n- `useState` — for simple state (flags, counters, for example).\\n\\n- `useReducer` — for complex state (forms, multi-step processes).\\n\\n- Redux — for global state and advanced scenarios.\",\"import { useReducer } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction counterReducer(state, action) {\\n    switch (action.type) {\\n        case 'increment':\\n            return { count: state.count + 1 };\\n        case 'decrement':\\n            return { count: state.count - 1 };\\n        default:\\n            return state;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n    const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(counterReducer, { count: 0 });\\n\\n    return (\\n        <div>\\n            <p>Count: {state.count}</p>\\n            <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: 'increment' })}>+</button>\\n            <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: 'decrement' })}>-</button>\\n        </div>\\n    );\\n}\\n\\nfunction formReducer(state, action) {\\n    switch (action.type) {\\n        case 'setUsername':\\n            return { ...state, username: action.payload };\\n        case 'setPassword':\\n            return { ...state, password: action.payload };\\n        case 'validate':\\n            return { ...state, errors: validateForm(state) };\\n        case 'submit':\\n            return { ...state, isSubmitting: true };\\n        default:\\n            return state;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nfunction Form() {\\n    const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(formReducer, {\\n        username: '',\\n        password: '',\\n        errors: {},\\n        isSubmitting: false,\\n   });\\n\\n    const handleSubmit = (e) => {\\n        e.preventDefault();\\n        dispatch({ type: 'validate' });\\n        if (Object.keys(state.errors).length === 0) {\\n            dispatch({ type: 'submit' });\\n        }\\n   };\\n\\n    return (\\n        <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>\\n            <input\\n              value={state.username}\\n              onChange={(e) => dispatch({ type: 'setUsername', payload: e.target.value })}\\n            />\\n            {state.errors.username && <p>{state.errors.username}</p>}\\n            <button disabled={state.isSubmitting}>Submit</button>\\n        </form>\\n    );\\n}\"],[2856,\"Flux is an architecture for managing state in applications. The data reaches the store as follows:\\n\\n- A component calls an action.\\n\\n- The Dispatcher receives the action and passes it to every store.\\n\\n- The store updates its data and notifies the components.\\n\\n- The components are repainted with the new data.\",\"The main parts of Flux\\n\\n- The Action — an object with a type (`type`) and data (`payload`), which describes what has happened (`{ type: 'ADD_TODO', text: '...' }`, for example).\\n\\n- The Dispatcher — the central «dispatcher», which passes an action to every store.\\n\\n- The Store — the data store (like the state in Redux). Listens for actions and updates itself.\\n\\n- The View (the React components) — displays the data from the store and starts the actions.\\n\\nHow does the data reach the store?\\n\\n- The user clicks a button in a component:\\n\\n- The component calls an action creator:\\n\\n- The Dispatcher sends the action to every store:\\n\\n- The components are subscribed to the store's changes and are updated:\\n\\nFlux vs Redux\\n\\n- In Redux there is one store, whereas in Flux there are several.\\n\\n- In Redux there is no Dispatcher; a reducer replaces it.\\n\\n- Flux is simpler for small applications, Redux for complex ones.\\n\\nAn example of the Flux architecture\\n\\n- `key`s in React have to be stable — random values break the update logic.\\n\\n- Flux is a one-directional flow of data: Action → Dispatcher → Store → View.\\n\\n- The store is updated only by way of actions, which makes the code predictable.\",\"<button onClick={() => addTodo('Buy milk')}>Add</button>\\n\\nfunction addTodo(text) {\\n  AppDispatcher.dispatch({\\n    type: 'ADD_TODO',\\n    text: text\\n  });\\n}\\n\\n// An example of a store (TodoStore)\\nTodoStore.dispatchToken = AppDispatcher.register(action => {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'ADD_TODO':\\n      _todos.push({ text: action.text, completed: false });\\n      TodoStore.emitChange(); // We notify the components\\n      break;\\n  }\\n});\\n\\nTodoStore.addChangeListener(this._onChange);\\n\\n// The Dispatcher\\nconst AppDispatcher = new Flux.Dispatcher();\\n// The Store\\nconst TodoStore = {\\n  _todos: [],\\n  emitChange() { /* Notifying the components */ },\\n  addChangeListener(callback) { /* The subscription */ }\\n};\\n// The Action\\nfunction addTodo(text) {\\n  AppDispatcher.dispatch({ type: 'ADD_TODO', text });\\n}\"],[2857,\"- Global variables — do not cause a re-render; the changes are hard to track.\\n\\n- Context — convenient for passing data without prop drilling.\\n\\n- A state manager (Redux, MobX) — predictability, time travel (debugging), scalability.\",\"1. The problem with global variables\\n\\nThe problems:\\n\\n- React will not learn that the variable has changed → there is no re-render.\\n\\n- The control of the dependencies is complex.\\n\\n2. The solution: React Context\\nPasses data through the tree of components without passing props explicitly.\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe pluses:\\n\\n- We avoid prop drilling (A → B → C → D).\\n\\n- Local state at the level of the application.\\n\\nThe minuses:\\n\\n- A re-render of every consumer when it changes.\\n\\n3. A state manager (Redux, MobX, Zustand)\\nAn example with Redux:\\n\\nWhat for?\\n\\n- Centralized state — a single source of truth.\\n\\n- Developer tools — logging, the rolling back of actions.\\n\\n- Optimizations — targeted subscriptions to the changes.\\n\\nConclusion:\\n\\n- For small projects Context will be enough.\\n\\n- For complex ones — Redux/Zustand.\\n\\n- Global variables — only for static data.\",\"let user = null;  \\n\\n// Component A  \\nuser = { name: 'Alice' };  \\n\\n// Component B  \\nconsole.log(user); // Sometimes current, sometimes not\\n\\nconst UserContext = createContext();  \\n\\nfunction App() {  \\n    const [user, setUser] = useState(null);  \\n    return (  \\n        <UserContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>  \\n            <Header />  \\n        </UserContext.Provider>  \\n    );  \\n}  \\n\\nfunction Header() {  \\n    const { user } = useContext(UserContext);  \\n    return <div>{user?.name}</div>;  \\n}\\n\\n// The store  \\nconst store = configureStore({ reducer: userReducer });  \\n\\n// The component  \\nconst user = useSelector(state => state.user);\"],[2858,\"An `enhancer` is a function that extends the capabilities of the Redux store — it adds middleware or developer tools, for example.\",\"The particulars:\\n\\n- A definition\\n\\n- An `enhancer` is a higher-order function for `createStore`.\\n\\n- Examples of its use\\n\\n- Connecting `redux-devtools`.\\n\\n- Applying middleware.\\n\\n- The syntax\\n\\nAn example with DevTools:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nEnhancers let you configure the store without changing the reducers' logic.\",\"const store = createStore(reducer, enhancer);\\n\\nconst store = createStore(\\n  reducer,\\n  window.__REDUX_DEVTOOLS_EXTENSION__ && window.__REDUX_DEVTOOLS_EXTENSION__()\\n);\"],[2859,\"You can use queues, middleware or deferred calls (`setTimeout`, `Promise`) in order to manage the order of the dispatches.\",\"The ways:\\n\\n- An asynchronous delay\\n\\n- Middleware\\n\\n- We wrap `dispatch` in logic with a queue.\\n\\n- Saga/Thunk\\n\\n- We manage the order through effects or a chain of promises.\\n\\nAn example of middleware:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nThe prioritization of dispatches is most often done at the level of the middleware or of the asynchronous actions.\",\"setTimeout(() => store.dispatch(action), 0);\\n\\nconst delayDispatch = store => next => action => {\\n  if (action.meta?.delay) {\\n    return setTimeout(() => next(action), action.meta.delay);\\n  }\\n  return next(action);\\n};\"],[2860,\"`redux-thunk` lets you dispatch functions for asynchronous logic; `redux-saga` lets you manage complex flows of events by means of generators.\",\"Redux Thunk:\\n\\n- Lets you dispatch a function `(dispatch, getState) => {}`.\\n\\n- Simple and intelligible for small projects.\\n\\nRedux Saga:\\n\\n- Works by way of generators.\\n\\n- Suits complex scenarios with side effects.\\n\\n- Is easy to test.\\n\\nAn example of Thunk:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nThunk is for simple asynchronous calls, Saga for complex business logic and the management of side effects.\",\"const fetchData = () => async dispatch => {\\n  dispatch({ type: 'loading' });\\n  const data = await api.get();\\n  dispatch({ type: 'success', payload: data });\\n};\"],[2861,\"Redux Toolkit provides predictable state management with immutable updates. MobX uses observable objects for reactive programming. React Query specializes in managing server state and caching.\",\"Redux Toolkit:\\n\\n- A centralized store of state\\n\\n- Immutable updates by way of reducers\\n\\n- Built-in support for asynchronous operations\\n\\n- Developer tools for debugging\\n\\nMobX:\\n\\n- Reactive programming\\n\\n- The automatic tracking of dependencies\\n\\n- Minimal boilerplate code\\n\\n- A simple learning curve\\n\\nReact Query:\\n\\n- A specialization in server state\\n\\n- Automatic caching and invalidation\\n\\n- The background updating of data\\n\\n- Pagination and infinite queries\\n\\nAn example of Redux Toolkit:\\n\\nWhen to use which:\\n\\n- Redux Toolkit for complex client state\\n\\n- MobX for reactive applications\\n\\n- React Query for working with an API and caching\",\"import { createSlice } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';\\n\\nconst counterSlice = createSlice({\\n  name: 'counter',\\n  initialState: 0,\\n  reducers: {\\n    increment: state => state + 1,\\n    decrement: state => state - 1\\n  }\\n});\"],[2862,\"React Context is a mechanism built into React for passing data through the tree of components without having to write props out at every level. Redux is an independent library for managing the state of a whole application, which provides strict rules for changing it (by way of actions and reducers) and powerful tools for debugging. Context solves the problem of \\\"prop drilling\\\", while Redux solves the problem of the predictable management of complex, global state.\",\"Both tools help you work with data, but they do so at different levels and with different aims.\\nReact Context\\n\\n- What it is: a mechanism for passing data right through. It lets you create a \\\"context\\\" (a source of data) at the top level and consume that data in any child component, wherever it may be, bypassing the intermediate components.\\n\\n- How it is used: it is created by means of `React.createContext()`. The provider (`<MyContext.Provider>`) wraps part of the tree and passes a value down. The consumer components use the `useContext(MyContext)` hook in order to access that value.\\n\\n- The main aim: avoiding \\\"prop drilling\\\" (the drilling of props through a multitude of unrelated components).\\n\\nRedux\\n\\n- What it is: a library for managing an application's state, based on the principles of Flux. It holds all of the state in a single immutable store.\\n\\n- How it is used: the state is changed only by way of actions and reducers — pure functions, which compute a new state predictably on the basis of the previous one and the action.\\n\\n- The main aim: to provide a predictable, centralized and debuggable way of managing complex state that is actively used in different parts of the application.\",null],[2863,\"Context is not intended for the frequent updating of large volumes of data. When a value in a context changes, React repaints every component that consumes it, even if they use only the part of the data that has not changed. Redux solves that problem by means of \\\"subscriptions\\\": a component is repainted only when precisely the data it is subscribed to changes. That gives an optimization of the performance in large applications\",\"Although Context copes excellently with passing static or rarely changing data (the UI theme or the user's data, for example), using it for frequently changing state has serious disadvantages.\\nThe problems with using Context for global state:\\n\\n- There are no selectors: a component using `useContext` is repainted on any change of the context's value, even if it needs only a small part of that data. There is no mechanism for subscribing to a particular field alone.\\n\\n- Complex optimization: in order to avoid superfluous repaints, you have to break the contexts up into smaller ones by hand (`UserContext`, `PostsContext`, `UiContext`) or use complex wrappers with `React.memo`. That increases the complexity of the code.\\n\\n- The state-update logic: in Context the state-update logic (analogous to the reducers in Redux) has to be implemented by hand, normally by means of `useState` or `useReducer` inside the provider.\\n\\nThe optimizations Redux gives:\\n\\n- A subscription to particular data: the `react-redux` library provides the `useSelector` hook, which lets a component subscribe only to a particular piece of state from the store. The component will be repainted only if that particular piece has changed.\\n\\n- The stability of the references: `useSelector` uses strict comparison (`===`) by default, which prevents superfluous repaints if the data has not changed.\\n\\n- A Single Source of Truth: Redux's architecture is predictable and makes debugging easier. It is easy to trace which action led to a change of state.\\n\\n- Middleware: Redux provides a powerful middleware mechanism (`redux-thunk`, `redux-saga`, for example) for handling side effects (asynchronous requests, logging and so on) in a uniform way.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nContext is an excellent tool for its own niche (data that is passed right through and changes rarely). Redux is a specialized, optimized library for managing the complex, frequently changing state of a large application, where performance and predictability are critical.\",null],[2864,\"When an action is dispatched in Redux, the following happens: the action is passed into the store, then through the middleware (if there is any), then to the root reducer, which passes it to every child reducer. Once the state has been updated, the subscribers (subscribe) are called, which notify the React components of the changes. The components are repainted with the new data.\",\"The dispatch mechanism in Redux represents a predictable flow of data through several stages.\\nThe complete dispatch process:\\n\\n- The initiation of the dispatch:\\n\\n- The call of `store.dispatch(action)`\\n\\n- The action has to be a plain JavaScript object with a `type` field\\n\\n- The handling by the middleware:\\n\\n- The action passes through the chain of middleware\\n\\n- Every middleware can change, log or cancel the action\\n\\n- Thunk middleware handles asynchronous operations\\n\\n- The call of the reducers:\\n\\n- The store passes the action to the root reducer\\n\\n- The root reducer delegates the handling to the corresponding child reducers\\n\\n- Every reducer receives the action, but handles only the types that correspond to it\\n\\nAn example of the flow of data:\\n\\n- Updating the state:\\n\\n- The reducers return a new state\\n\\n- The store replaces the current state with the new one\\n\\n- The references to the state objects change only on real changes\\n\\n- Notifying the subscribers:\\n\\n- The store calls every subscriber function\\n\\n- The React components receive updated props\\n\\n- A re-render of the components affected happens\",\"// 1. Creating the action\\nconst action = { type: 'USER_LOGGED_IN', payload: { userId: 123 } };\\n\\n// 2. The dispatch\\nstore.dispatch(action);\\n\\n// 3. The middleware (the example of redux-thunk)\\nconst thunkMiddleware = store => next => action => {\\n  if (typeof action === 'function') {\\n    return action(store.dispatch, store.getState);\\n  }\\n  return next(action);\\n};\\n\\n// 4. The reducer\\nconst rootReducer = (state = {}, action) => {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'USER_LOGGED_IN':\\n      return { ...state, user: action.payload };\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n};\"],[2865,\"A reducer in Redux serves to determine how the application's state changes in response to actions. It is a pure function, which takes the current state and an action and returns a new state. Reducers have to be immutable — they do not change the original state but return a new object with the updated data.\",\"A reducer is the central concept of Redux, responsible for the transformation of an application's state.\\nThe main characteristics of reducers:\\nThe purity of the function:\\n\\n- They must have no side effects\\n\\n- They must not change their arguments\\n\\n- They must not perform asynchronous operations\\n\\n- They must return the same result for the same arguments\\n\\nImmutability:\\n\\n- They always return a new state object\\n\\n- They do not change the existing objects and arrays\\n\\n- They use the spread operator or libraries such as Immer for the updates\\n\\nThe structure of a reducer:\\n\\nThe role in Redux's architecture:\\nManaging the state:\\n\\n- Defines the possible changes of state\\n\\n- Ensures the predictability of the changes\\n\\n- Centralizes the state-update logic\\n\\nThe separation of responsibility:\\n\\n- Every reducer is responsible for its own part of the state\\n\\n- Combining them through combineReducers\\n\\n- The ease of testing and debugging\\n\\nThe advantages of the approach:\\n\\n- The predictability of the changes of state\\n\\n- The ease of debugging (time-travel debugging)\\n\\n- The simplicity of testing\\n\\n- The scalability of the application\",\"// An example of a reducer\\nconst initialState = {\\n  items: [],\\n  loading: false,\\n  error: null\\n};\\n\\nconst itemsReducer = (state = initialState, action) => {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'ITEMS_LOADING':\\n      return {\\n        ...state,\\n        loading: true,\\n        error: null\\n      };\\n    case 'ITEMS_LOADED':\\n      return {\\n        ...state,\\n        items: action.payload,\\n        loading: false\\n      };\\n    case 'ITEMS_ERROR':\\n      return {\\n        ...state,\\n        loading: false,\\n        error: action.payload\\n      };\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n};\"],[2866,\"Redux cannot work without reducers — when the store is created, a root reducer always has to be passed in. If you pass undefined or null as the reducer, Redux will throw an error at the stage where the store is created. In development mode Redux performs additional checks and warns you of improper configuration.\",\"Redux has strict requirements for its configuration and points to problems with the configuration explicitly.\\nThe process of initializing the store:\\nCreating the store:\\n\\n- The `createStore` function requires a mandatory `reducer` parameter\\n\\n- In the absence of a reducer, an error arises at the stage where the function is called\\n\\n- Redux does not create a store by default without a reducer being specified explicitly\\n\\nExamples of erroneous scenarios:\\n\\nValidation in development mode:\\nRedux's checks:\\n\\n- The validation of the type of the reducer passed in (it has to be a function)\\n\\n- A check of the initial state at the first call\\n\\n- Warnings about possible problems with the configuration\\n\\nThe correct configuration:\\n\\nHandling the edge cases:\\nAn empty reducer:\\n\\n- A reducer may handle no actions at all\\n\\n- But it always has to return the state\\n\\n- The initial state is mandatory, by way of the default parameter\\n\\nCombined reducers:\\n\\n- `combineReducers` checks that every reducer passed in is a function\\n\\n- Creates a root reducer, which delegates the actions\\n\\n- Even when combining a single reducer, it has to be a valid one\",\"// AN ERROR: the reducer has not been specified\\nconst store = createStore(); // TypeError\\n\\n// AN ERROR: the reducer is undefined\\nconst store = createStore(undefined); // An Error in development\\n\\n// AN ERROR: an incorrect reducer\\nconst store = createStore('not_a_function'); // An Error\\n\\n// A minimal working reducer\\nconst rootReducer = (state = {}, action) => {\\n  return state;\\n};\\n\\n// Creating the store with a valid reducer\\nconst store = createStore(rootReducer);\"],[2867,\"Before being handled in the reducers, an action passes through every middleware installed in the store. Middleware can modify, log, delay or cancel an action entirely. Side effects and asynchronous operations can also be performed through middleware such as redux-thunk or redux-saga.\",\"Between the dispatching of an action and its handling in the reducers, several important stages happen.\\nThe process of handling an action:\\n\\n- The dispatching of the action:\\n\\n- The call of `store.dispatch(action)`\\n\\n- The start of the handling process\\n\\n- The chain of middleware:\\n\\n- The action passes through every middleware one after another\\n\\n- Every middleware receives a `next` function for passing the action further on\\n\\n- Middleware can perform various operations on the action\\n\\nAn example of a middleware chain:\\n\\nThe types of operation in middleware:\\nModifying the action:\\n\\n- Adding additional data\\n\\n- Transforming the action's structure\\n\\n- Creating several actions out of one\\n\\nAsynchronous operations:\\n\\n- API calls before the action is passed on\\n\\n- Delaying the dispatch\\n\\n- A conditional dispatch based on the current state\\n\\nMonitoring and logging:\\n\\n- Tracing the actions for debugging\\n\\n- Performance metrics\\n\\n- Error handling\\n\\nAfter the middleware:\\n\\n- The action is passed into the reducers only if it has not been cancelled\\n\\n- Every middleware has to call `next(action)` in order to continue the chain\\n\\n- The middleware's result may be a modified action\",\"// Simple logging middleware\\nconst loggerMiddleware = store => next => action => {\\n  console.log('Dispatching:', action);\\n  const result = next(action);\\n  console.log('Next state:', store.getState());\\n  return result;\\n};\\n\\n// Thunk middleware for asynchronous operations\\nconst thunkMiddleware = store => next => action => {\\n  if (typeof action === 'function') {\\n    return action(store.dispatch, store.getState);\\n  }\\n  return next(action);\\n};\\n\\n// Applying the middleware\\nconst store = createStore(\\n  rootReducer,\\n  applyMiddleware(thunkMiddleware, loggerMiddleware)\\n);\"],[2868,\"`rootReducer` passes every action to ALL of the child reducers, independently of the number of reducers and of whether the action's type matches. Every reducer receives the action and decides for itself whether it needs to update its state. In a switch/case, the default branch returns the current state unchanged.\",\"Redux uses a pattern in which actions are broadcast to every reducer rather than being handled on a first-match principle.\\nThe mechanism of combineReducers:\\nCreating the root reducer:\\n\\n- `combineReducers` creates a function that calls every child reducer\\n\\n- Every reducer receives the very same copy of the action\\n\\n- The reducers work independently of one another\\n\\nAn example of the structure:\\n\\nHandling an action:\\nFor the action `USER_UPDATE`:\\n\\n- `userReducer` handles the action and returns a new state\\n\\n- `postsReducer` receives the same action but returns the current state (the default case)\\n\\n- The root reducer combines the results\\n\\nFor the action `POST_ADD`:\\n\\n- `userReducer` returns the current state (the default case)\\n\\n- `postsReducer` handles the action and returns a new state\\n\\nThe advantages of the approach:\\nFlexibility:\\n\\n- Several reducers can respond to one type of action\\n\\n- It is easy to add new functionality without changing the existing reducers\\n\\n- The predictability of the system's behaviour\\n\\nPerformance:\\n\\n- Redux is optimized for passing through every reducer fast\\n\\n- Most reducers return the state quickly in the default case\\n\\n- Various optimizations are used in production\\n\\nRecommendations:\\n\\n- Keep the reducers simple and focused on their own area\\n\\n- Use the default case in order to return the state unchanged\\n\\n- Avoid complex logic in the reducers\",\"const userReducer = (state = {}, action) => {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'USER_UPDATE':\\n      return { ...state, ...action.payload };\\n    default:\\n      return state; // The state unchanged, for the other actions\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nconst postsReducer = (state = [], action) => {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'POST_ADD':\\n      return [...state, action.payload];\\n    default:\\n      return state; // The state unchanged, for the other actions\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nconst rootReducer = combineReducers({\\n  user: userReducer,\\n  posts: postsReducer\\n});\"],[2869,\"Middleware runs after `dispatch()` has been called but before the action reaches the reducers. It sits in the middle of the dispatch process, between the initiation of the action and the actual update of the state. Every middleware in the chain receives the action and decides whether to pass it further on, modify it or cancel it.\",\"Middleware in Redux is run in a strictly defined sequence between the dispatching of an action and its handling in the reducers.\\nThe order of execution:\\n\\n- The dispatching of the action: `store.dispatch(action)`\\n\\n- The handling in the middleware: the action passes through the whole chain of middleware\\n\\n- Reaching the reducers: after all of the middleware, the action reaches the reducers\\n\\n- Updating the state: the reducers return a new state\\n\\nThe detailed process of the middleware:\\n\\nThe key aspects:\\n\\n- Before next: the code runs BEFORE the action is passed to the next middleware/the reducers\\n\\n- After next: the code runs AFTER all of the subsequent middleware and the reducers have finished their work\\n\\n- Control of the flow: middleware can stop the passing of the action further on entirely\",\"// An example of a chain of middleware\\nconst middleware1 = store => next => action => {\\n  console.log('Middleware 1 before next');\\n  const result = next(action);\\n  console.log('Middleware 1 after next');\\n  return result;\\n};\\n\\nconst middleware2 = store => next => action => {\\n  console.log('Middleware 2 before next');\\n  const result = next(action);\\n  console.log('Middleware 2 after next');\\n  return result;\\n};\\n\\n// The order of execution on a dispatch:\\n// 1. \\\"Middleware 1 before next\\\"\\n// 2. \\\"Middleware 2 before next\\\" \\n// 3. The reducers handle the action\\n// 4. \\\"Middleware 2 after next\\\"\\n// 5. \\\"Middleware 1 after next\\\"\"],[2870,\"To prioritize dispatches you can use `createListenerMiddleware` from Redux Toolkit, which lets you defer the execution of actions. You can also use Promise-based approaches or custom middleware, which accumulates the actions and performs them in the order needed. The simplest way is to dispatch the last action in a callback, once the previous operations have finished.\",\"There are several approaches for ensuring that actions are performed in a particular order.\\nThe methods of prioritization:\\n1. The createListenerMiddleware approach:\\n\\n2. The Promise-based approach:\\n\\n3. A custom queue of actions:\\n\\nA practical example:\\n\\nRecommendations:\\n\\n- Use the official `createListenerMiddleware` for complex scenarios\\n\\n- For simple cases a `setTimeout` with a 0 delay is enough\\n\\n- Avoid blocking operations in the main thread\",\"import { createListenerMiddleware } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';\\n\\nconst listenerMiddleware = createListenerMiddleware();\\n\\n// A listener for a particular action\\nlistenerMiddleware.startListening({\\n  actionCreator: someAction,\\n  effect: async (action, listenerApi) => {\\n    // We wait for the other operations to finish\\n    await listenerApi.condition((action, currentState) => {\\n      return currentState.someCondition === true;\\n    });\\n    \\n    // Then we dispatch the final action\\n    listenerApi.dispatch(finalAction());\\n  }\\n});\\n\\n// A dispatch that is always performed last\\nconst dispatchLast = (action) => (dispatch, getState) => {\\n  return Promise.resolve().then(() => {\\n    dispatch(action);\\n  });\\n};\\n\\n// Using it\\nstore.dispatch(dispatchLast(myAction()));\\n\\nconst actionQueueMiddleware = store => next => action => {\\n  if (action.meta?.priority === 'last') {\\n    // We defer the execution until the next tick\\n    setTimeout(() => {\\n      next(action);\\n    }, 0);\\n    return;\\n  }\\n  return next(action);\\n};\\n\\n// Middleware for a deferred dispatch\\nconst deferredDispatchMiddleware = store => {\\n  let pendingActions = [];\\n  let isProcessing = false;\\n\\n  return next => action => {\\n    if (action.type === 'DEFERRED_ACTION') {\\n      pendingActions.push(action.payload);\\n      \\n      if (!isProcessing) {\\n        isProcessing = true;\\n        // We perform them in the next tick of the event loop\\n        setTimeout(() => {\\n          pendingActions.forEach(deferredAction => {\\n            store.dispatch(deferredAction);\\n          });\\n          pendingActions = [];\\n          isProcessing = false;\\n        }, 0);\\n      }\\n      return;\\n    }\\n    return next(action);\\n  };\\n};\"],[2871,\"The time of a click on a button in Redux is best logged by means of middleware. Middleware is the point through which every action passes before it reaches the reducer. Inside the middleware you can intercept the action you need (`'BUTTON_CLICKED'`, for example) and log the current time (`console.log(Date.now())`, or send it to a logging service). It is a centralized and consistent approach.\",\"In Redux's architecture the data flows in one direction: `View -> Action -> Reducer -> Store -> View`. Middleware provides a powerful mechanism for extending that flow.\\nWhy is middleware the best place?\\n\\n- The separation of responsibility: the logging logic pollutes neither the components (the UI) nor the reducers (the state-update logic).\\n\\n- Centralization: you can log every action in one place rather than looking for them throughout the application's code.\\n\\n- Access to the complete action: the middleware has access to the `action` object, which lets you log not only the time but the payload too.\\n\\nHow do you implement it?\\n\\n- Create custom middleware:\\njavascript\\n\\n- Connect the middleware to the store:\\njavascript\\n\\nAlternative (less preferable) places:\\n\\n- In the component (in the click handler): violates the principle of the separation of responsibility, makes testing more complex.\\n\\n- In the reducer: reducers have to be pure functions, and logging is a side effect.\\n\\nConclusion\\nTo log actions, including the time of clicks, use Redux's middleware. It is the standard, maintainable approach, and the cleanest from an architectural point of view.\",\"const loggerMiddleware = store => next => action => {\\n  // We log the time and the action BEFORE it is handled by the reducer\\n  if (action.type === 'BUTTON_CLICKED') {\\n    console.log('Button clicked at:', new Date().toISOString());\\n  }\\n  // We pass the action to the next middleware in the chain, or to the reducer\\n  return next(action);\\n};\\n\\nimport { createStore, applyMiddleware } from 'redux';\\nimport rootReducer from './reducers';\\n\\nconst store = createStore(\\n  rootReducer,\\n  applyMiddleware(loggerMiddleware) // and other middleware...\\n);\"],[2872,\"To memoize a selector call in Redux, the Reselect library is used. It lets you create \\\"memoized\\\" selectors, which recompute their value only when the selector's input data (its arguments) changes. If the arguments have stayed the same, the selector returns the cached result, which prevents expensive computations and superfluous re-renders of the components.\",\"Memoization is an optimization technique that caches the results of expensive function calls.\\nThe problem without memoization\\nImagine a selector that filters and sorts a large list:\\n\\nIf the component connects to the store by means of `connect`, that selector will be run on every update of the Redux state, even if that update is unrelated to `todos` or `visibilityFilter`.\\nThe solution with Reselect\\nReselect provides the `createSelector` function for creating memoized selectors.\\n\\n- Install Reselect: `npm install reselect`\\n\\n- Create the memoized selector:\\njavascript\\n\\nHow it works\\nOn the first call of `getVisibleTodos(state)`, Reselect runs the transformer function and caches the result. On the subsequent calls it first checks whether the results of `getTodos(state)` and `getVisibilityFilter(state)` have changed. If not, it returns the cached value.\\nConclusion\\nUse Reselect in order to memoize the selectors that perform complex computations or derived transformations on the state. That raises the performance considerably, especially in large applications, by cutting down the number of unnecessary computations and re-renders of the components involved.\",\"const getVisibleTodos = (state) => {\\n  // This computation is performed on EVERY call of the selector,\\n  // even if `state.todos` and `state.visibilityFilter` have not changed.\\n  const { todos, visibilityFilter } = state;\\n  return todos.filter(todo => {\\n    switch (visibilityFilter) {\\n      case 'SHOW_COMPLETED':\\n        return todo.completed;\\n      case 'SHOW_ACTIVE':\\n        return !todo.completed;\\n      default:\\n        return true;\\n    }\\n  });\\n};\\n\\nimport { createSelector } from 'reselect';\\n\\n// The simple selectors, which merely extract a piece of the state\\nconst getTodos = state => state.todos;\\nconst getVisibilityFilter = state => state.visibilityFilter;\\n\\n// The memoized selector\\nexport const getVisibleTodos = createSelector(\\n  [getTodos, getVisibilityFilter], // The array of input selectors\\n  (todos, visibilityFilter) => { // The transformer function\\n    // The expensive computation will be performed ONLY if todos or visibilityFilter has changed\\n    return todos.filter(todo => {\\n      switch (visibilityFilter) {\\n        case 'SHOW_COMPLETED':\\n          return todo.completed;\\n        case 'SHOW_ACTIVE':\\n          return !todo.completed;\\n        default:\\n          return true;\\n      }\\n    });\\n  }\\n);\"],[2873,\"Redux gives a stricter, more predictable model of state management.\\nIt keeps the updates and the re-renders under better control.\\nRedux scales better than Context.\\nIt is therefore chosen more often for large applications.\",\"Context and Redux solve a similar task, but at a different level of complexity.\\nA definition\\nRedux is a centralized state manager with an explicit flow of data and strict rules for changing the state.\\nThe problems with Context in large applications\\nAs an application grows:\\n\\n- Context starts updating often\\n\\n- it is hard to keep the dependencies under control\\n\\n- it is difficult to optimize the re-renders\\n\\n- the state-change logic gets smeared about\\n\\nThe advantages of Redux\\nRedux provides:\\n\\n- a single source of truth\\n\\n- explicit `action`s\\n\\n- predictable `reducer`s\\n\\n- convenient debugging and logging\\n\\nThe changes of state become transparent and traceable.\\nControl of the re-renders\\nRedux:\\n\\n- lets you subscribe only to the data you need\\n\\n- minimizes superfluous updates\\n\\n- suits performant UIs better\\n\\nWhen Context is better all the same\\nContext remains a good choice for:\\n\\n- the theme\\n\\n- the localization\\n\\n- the current user\\n\\n- global data that changes rarely\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nRedux is preferred in large applications because of its predictability, its scalability and its control of the updates. Context is convenient but has architectural limitations.\",null],[2874,\"A reducer is a function that describes how an application's state changes.\\nIt takes the current state and an action.\\nA reducer always returns a new state.\\nSide effects are forbidden inside a reducer.\",\"A reducer is the heart of Redux; it is precisely what defines the state-change logic.\\nA definition\\nA reducer is a pure function of the form `(state, action) => newState`, which describes how the state is to change in response to an action.\\nHow a reducer works\\nThe algorithm is simple:\\n\\n- Redux passes the current `state` in\\n\\n- Redux passes the `action` in\\n\\n- The reducer analyses the type of the action\\n\\n- A new state is returned\\n\\nWhy a reducer has to be pure\\nA reducer:\\n\\n- does not change the `state` directly\\n\\n- does not call an API\\n\\n- does not work with timers\\n\\n- always returns the same result for the same input data\\n\\nThat makes the application's behaviour predictable.\\nThe reducer's role in the architecture\\nA reducer:\\n\\n- centralizes the business logic\\n\\n- makes testing easier\\n\\n- makes the changes of state transparent\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nA reducer is a pure function describing how the state changes over time. It is the foundation of Redux's predictability.\",\"function counterReducer(state = 0, action) {\\n  if (action.type === 'increment') {\\n    return state + 1;\\n  }\\n  return state;\\n}\"],[2875,\"Redux Toolkit makes working with Redux easier.\\nIt reduces the amount of boilerplate code.\\nRedux Toolkit encourages the best practices.\\nToday it is the recommended way of using Redux.\",\"Classic Redux requires a lot of repetitive code, which often leads to errors.\\nA definition\\nRedux Toolkit is the official set of tools that makes the configuration and use of Redux easier.\\nThe main differences\\nRedux Toolkit:\\n\\n- configures the `store` automatically\\n\\n- uses `createSlice`\\n\\n- lets you write «mutable» code safely\\n\\n- includes useful middleware by default\\n\\nWhy «mutable» code is safe\\nRedux Toolkit uses `Immer`, which:\\n\\n- lets you write code as though with mutations\\n\\n- creates immutable copies under the bonnet\\n\\nThe problems with classic Redux\\nIn the classic approach:\\n\\n- a lot of `action type`s\\n\\n- a lot of `switch`es\\n\\n- a high likelihood of errors\\n\\n- the code is harder to maintain\\n\\nWhen to use Redux Toolkit\\nToday:\\n\\n- Redux Toolkit is used almost always\\n\\n- classic Redux is met with rarely\\n\\n- Toolkit is considered the standard\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nRedux Toolkit makes Redux simpler, safer and more modern. It is the recommended approach for new projects.\",\"const slice = createSlice({\\n  name: 'counter',\\n  initialState: 0,\\n  reducers: {\\n    increment: state => state + 1\\n  }\\n});\"],[2876,\"Selectors are called on any change of the store, because Redux does not know which data has really changed.\\nThe store tells its subscribers about any update of the state.\\nThe selectors themselves decide whether the fragment they need has changed.\\nThat is normal Redux behaviour.\",\"Redux uses a broad notification model rather than targeted events.\\nA definition\\nA selector is a function that extracts part of the data from the state of the Redux store.\\nHow an update of the store works\\nOn every `dispatch`:\\n\\n- the reducer returns a new state\\n\\n- the store notifies every subscriber\\n\\n- every subscriber calls its selectors again\\n\\nRedux does not analyse which parts of the state have changed.\\nWhy it is arranged that way\\nSuch an approach:\\n\\n- makes Redux's implementation simpler\\n\\n- makes the behaviour predictable\\n\\n- shifts the optimization onto the level of the selectors\\n\\nThe role of memoization\\nIn order to avoid superfluous computations:\\n\\n- memoized selectors are used\\n\\n- `reselect` is applied\\n\\nMemoization lets you:\\n\\n- return the old value\\n\\n- prevent a superfluous re-render\\n\\nThe connection with React\\nIn React:\\n\\n- `useSelector` compares the selector's results\\n\\n- where they are equal by reference, no re-render happens\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nSelectors are always called, but the re-render depends on the result. The optimization is achieved by the memoization of the selectors.\",null],[2877,\"State managers live outside React, because they do not depend on its life cycle and can be used without React at all. React merely subscribes to the changes in those stores. Such an approach lets you separate the management of the state from the display of the UI. That is precisely why they are called an external store.\",\"React and state managers solve different tasks.\\nWhat React does\\nBefore the enumeration it matters to fix its role:\\n\\n- React is responsible for rendering the UI.\\n\\n- React responds to changes of `state` and `props`.\\n\\n- React does not manage the application's global state.\\n\\nWhat a state manager does\\n\\n- Holds the state outside the components.\\n\\n- Provides an API for reading and changing the data.\\n\\n- Notifies its subscribers of the changes.\\n\\nReact in that process:\\n\\n- Does not know how the state is held.\\n\\n- Does not know who changes it.\\n\\n- Only responds to the notifications.\\n\\nWhy that is architecturally right\\n\\n- A separation of responsibility\\n\\n- the UI separately, the business logic separately.\\n\\n- Reusability\\n\\n- the store can be used outside React (in tests, for example).\\n\\n- Several consumers\\n\\n- one store can serve several React trees.\\n\\nConclusion\\nState managers are considered external with respect to React because they are an independent source of state, while React merely integrates with them through subscriptions.\",\"store.setState(nextState);\"],[2878,\"Redux is built on the Flux architectural pattern, with a one-directional flow of data. The state is held in a single store; the changes happen only by way of actions and reducers. The components do not change the state directly but merely initiate events. That makes the application's behaviour predictable and convenient for debugging.\",\"Redux is not merely a library but a particular architectural model.\\nA definition\\nA definition: Flux is an architectural pattern with a one-directional flow of data, in which the state is changed centrally and predictably.\\nHow Flux is implemented in Redux\\n\\n- The Action\\n\\n- Describes what has happened\\n\\n- An ordinary object with a `type` and a payload\\n\\n- The Reducer\\n\\n- A pure function\\n\\n- Takes the `state` and an `action`\\n\\n- Returns a new `state`\\n\\n- The Store\\n\\n- The single source of truth\\n\\n- Holds all of the application's state\\n\\nThe one-directional flow of data\\n\\n- The UI calls `dispatch`\\n\\n- The action reaches the reducer\\n\\n- The reducer returns a new state\\n\\n- The UI is subscribed to the store's changes and is repainted\\n\\nSuch a flow rules out “hidden” changes of state.\\nWhy that matters\\n\\n- The state cannot be changed by accident\\n\\n- The behaviour is easy to reproduce\\n\\n- The reducers are simpler to test\\n\\nConclusion\\nRedux follows the Flux approach: one-directional data + pure reducers, which makes the state transparent and manageable.\",\"dispatch({ type: \\\"user/login\\\", payload: user });\"],[2879,\"Time-travel debugging is the ability to “wind” an application's state backwards and forwards. Redux holds a history of the actions, and since reducers are deterministic, any state can be reproduced. That lets you see which action led to an error. It is normally implemented through Redux DevTools.\",\"Time travel is a direct consequence of Redux's architecture.\\nA definition\\nA definition: time-travel debugging is a debugging technique in which an application's state is reproduced by way of applying the actions one after another.\\nWhy it is possible at all\\n\\n- The state is immutable\\n\\n- Every action creates a new state\\n\\n- The reducers are pure functions\\n\\n- The same input → the same output\\n\\n- The actions are events\\n\\n- They can be held and replayed\\n\\nWhat it looks like in practice\\n\\n- The user clicks a button\\n\\n- The action appears in DevTools\\n\\n- You can:\\n\\n- roll back to any step\\n\\n- look at the diff of the state\\n\\n- repeat the chain of actions\\n\\nWhat that gives the developer\\n\\n- The search for complex bugs\\n\\n- An understanding of the application's logic\\n\\n- The ability to reproduce a bug from the log of actions\\n\\nThe limitations\\n\\n- A large state → a lot of memory\\n\\n- Side effects (an API) are not “wound back”\\n\\n- In production it is normally switched off\\n\\nConclusion\\nTime-travel debugging works because Redux makes the state deterministic and reproducible rather than “magical”.\",null],[2880,\"Time travel is called a killer feature because it makes debugging radically easier. The developer sees the whole history of the changes of state and can understand exactly where the logic went wrong. That matters especially in complex interfaces with a large number of states. Such a level of transparency is rarely available in other approaches.\",\"Redux is valued not only for its architecture but for its tools.\\nWhy it really is a “killer feature”\\n1. The complete transparency of the state\\n\\n- Every change is visible\\n\\n- It is clear which action fired and when\\n\\n- There are no “hidden” mutations\\n\\n2. The repeatability of bugs\\n\\n- The sequence of actions can be saved\\n\\n- A bug can be reproduced in another environment\\n\\n- The communication within the team is made easier\\n\\n3. Speeding development up\\n\\n- Fewer `console.log`s\\n\\n- A faster analysis of complex scenarios\\n\\n- An easier onboarding of new developers\\n\\n4. Architectural discipline\\n\\n- It forces you to write pure reducers\\n\\n- It teaches you to think in events rather than mutations\\n\\n- It makes the logic predictable\\n\\nConclusion\\nTime travel is Redux's killer feature, because it turns the state from a “black box” into a transparent, manageable history, which greatly raises the quality and the speed of development.\",null],[2881,\"Redux and MobX solve the same task — the management of state — but they do so differently. Redux is based on explicit events, immutability and predictability. MobX stakes everything on reactivity and the automatic tracking of dependencies. Redux is chosen more often for large, complex applications, MobX for the fast, convenient development of a UI.\",\"The main difference between Redux and MobX is the philosophy of state management.\\nA definition\\nA definition: Redux is an event-oriented store with a one-directional flow of data; MobX is a reactive state system with the automatic tracking of dependencies.\\nHow Redux thinks\\nRedux forces you to describe what has happened.\\n\\n- The state is immutable\\n\\n- Any change is by way of an `action`\\n\\n- All of the logic is concentrated in the reducers\\n\\n- The update of the UI is a consequence of the change of the store\\n\\nThe consequences:\\n\\n- The behaviour is easy to reproduce\\n\\n- Excellent debugging\\n\\n- More boilerplate code\\n\\nHow MobX thinks\\nMobX focuses on the data and its use.\\n\\n- The state can be mutated directly\\n\\n- The components subscribe automatically to the data they use\\n\\n- There is no explicit division into action / reducer\\n\\n- The changes are “pushed” reactively\\n\\nThe consequences:\\n\\n- Less code\\n\\n- A fast start\\n\\n- The chain of changes is harder to trace\\n\\nThe key differences in practice\\n\\n- Predictability\\n\\n- Redux: high\\n\\n- MobX: depends on your discipline\\n\\n- Debugging\\n\\n- Redux: time travel, a history of the events\\n\\n- MobX: reactive updates without an explicit log\\n\\n- The threshold of entry\\n\\n- Redux: higher\\n\\n- MobX: lower\\n\\n- Scaling\\n\\n- Redux: good for large teams\\n\\n- MobX: excellent for small and medium projects\\n\\nConclusion\\nRedux is chosen when control, predictability and debugging matter; MobX when a fast, convenient reactive UI with a minimum of boilerplate code is needed.\",\"dispatch({ type: \\\"counter/increment\\\" });\\n\\nstore.count++;\"],[2882,\"MobX is built on the Observer pattern and on the principles of reactive programming. The state is declared as observable, and the components become observers automatically. When the data changes, MobX itself decides which parts of the UI need updating. The developer does not have to describe the links between the state and the view by hand.\",\"MobX uses a reactive model, close to spreadsheet logic.\\nA definition\\nA definition: the observer pattern is the pattern in which objects are notified automatically of changes to the data they are subscribed to.\\nHow it is implemented in MobX\\n\\n- Observable\\n\\n- The data that is being observed\\n\\n- Observer\\n\\n- The components or functions that use that data\\n\\n- Automatic tracking\\n\\n- MobX itself remembers which observables have been read\\n\\n- `count` is an observable\\n\\n- A component reading `count` becomes an observer\\n\\n- When `count` changes, the UI is updated automatically\\n\\nHow that differs from the Flux approach\\n\\n- There is no explicit event (`action`)\\n\\n- There is no centralized reducer\\n\\n- The links are determined at run time\\n\\nThe pluses and the minuses of the pattern\\n\\n- The pluses\\n\\n- A minimum of code\\n\\n- An intuitive model\\n\\n- High performance thanks to the targeted updates\\n\\n- The minuses\\n\\n- Complex scenarios are harder to debug\\n\\n- It is not always obvious where a change came from\\n\\nConclusion\\nMobX is based on Observer + reactivity, which makes it a powerful, convenient tool, but it calls for discipline if the application is to remain intelligible and maintainable.\",\"const store = makeAutoObservable({\\n  count: 0,\\n  inc() {\\n    this.count++;\\n  },\\n});\"],[2883,\"`Thunk` is simple middleware that lets you write asynchronous logic right in the actions. `Saga` is a separate layer of logic based on generators, which manages side effects declaratively. `Thunk` is simpler to master but scales worse. `Saga` is more complex, but suits complex scenarios and business logic better.\",\"The general idea\\nRedux is synchronous in itself, so middleware is used for asynchronous logic.\\nRedux Thunk\\nThunk lets an action be a function.\\n\\nThe characteristics:\\n\\n- A minimal threshold of entry\\n\\n- The logic and the actions are mixed\\n\\n- Good for simple requests\\n\\n- Complex scenarios are hard to test\\n\\nRedux Saga\\nSaga uses generators in order to describe the effects.\\n\\nThe characteristics:\\n\\n- A clear separation of the logic and the actions\\n\\n- The declarative description of the effects\\n\\n- Convenient for complex scenarios\\n\\n- Tests well\\n\\n- A higher threshold of entry\\n\\nThe key differences in approach\\n\\n- Thunk is imperative\\n\\n- Saga is declarative\\n\\n- Thunk is simpler for beginners to read\\n\\n- Saga scales better\\n\\nConclusion\\n`Thunk` is the fast, simple choice for small projects. `Saga` is justified when the logic is complex and asynchronous and requires strict control.\",\"const fetchData = () => (dispatch) => {\\n  dispatch(start());\\n  fetch(url)\\n    .then(r => r.json())\\n    .then(data => dispatch(success(data)));\\n};\\n\\nfunction* fetchDataSaga() {\\n  try {\\n    const data = yield call(api.fetch);\\n    yield put(success(data));\\n  } catch (e) {\\n    yield put(error(e));\\n  }\\n}\"],[2884,\"MobX is harder to debug because of its implicit dependencies. The components subscribe to the observable data automatically, and it is not always obvious what exactly caused an update. Unlike Redux, where the flow of data is strictly deterministic, in MobX the reactivity happens “magically”. That makes tracing the changes more complex.\",\"The key cause of the difficulty\\nMobX is built on automatic reactivity.\\nWhat happens under the bonnet\\n\\n- The observable data is tracked automatically\\n\\n- A component subscribes to everything it uses\\n\\n- Any change causes a recomputation of the dependent parts\\n\\nWhy that makes debugging more complex\\n\\n- There are no explicit actions as a single point of entry\\n\\n- It is hard to understand which change caused a re-render\\n\\n- The dependencies are formed dynamically\\n\\n- The behaviour may differ from your expectations\\n\\nA comparison with Redux (conceptually)\\n\\n- Redux:\\n\\n- explicit actions\\n\\n- one store\\n\\n- an intelligible data flow\\n\\n- MobX:\\n\\n- mutable data\\n\\n- reactive links\\n\\n- less boilerplate code, more “magic”\\n\\nWhen MobX is justified all the same\\n\\n- Small projects\\n\\n- Prototypes\\n\\n- Teams with a good understanding of reactivity\\n\\nConclusion\\nMobX is faster to develop in but harder to debug, because of its implicit reactivity. Redux is simpler to analyse and to maintain in large teams.\",null],[2885,\"Global state holds the data that is used in several parts of the application. Local state suits the UI details of a particular component. The wider the area in which the data is used, the higher the likelihood that it belongs in the global store. Superfluous global state makes maintenance more complex. The decision is always taken on the basis of the data's real use.\",\"What global state is\\nGlobal state is the data available to several independent parts of an application at once. It is used in order to synchronize the behaviour of different components.\\nThe main criteria for moving data into global state\\nBefore making data global, the following factors are normally assessed:\\n\\n- The area of its use\\n\\n- The data is needed in several screens\\n\\n- The data is used by components with no direct hierarchical link\\n\\n- Longevity\\n\\n- The state has to be preserved across navigation\\n\\n- The data is not tied to the life cycle of a single component\\n\\n- The semantics of the data\\n\\n- The user\\n\\n- The access rights\\n\\n- The global settings\\n\\nWhat you should not hold in global state\\n\\n- The state of UI elements\\n\\n- Temporary flags and local toggles\\n\\n- Data used in only one component\\n\\nAn example of local state:\\n\\nThe typical mistake\\nA widespread problem is moving everything there is into global state «for the future». That leads to a complication of the logic and a growth in the coupling.\\nConclusion\\nGlobal state should contain only data that really is shared and significant. Everything else is safer and simpler to hold locally.\",\"const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false)\"],[2886,\"Excessive global state makes an application's logic more complex. The code becomes more coupled, and changes become risky. The complexity of debugging and testing rises. Superfluous repaints of components arise. The result is that the application becomes harder to maintain and to develop.\",\"Global state is a powerful tool, but when applied improperly it starts to harm the architecture.\\nThe main problems with excessive global state\\n\\n- A growth in coupling\\n\\n- The components start depending on a shared store\\n\\n- A change of state in one place affects a multitude of screens\\n\\n- The complexity of debugging\\n\\n- It is hard to understand who changed the data and why\\n\\n- The state-update logic gets smeared across the project\\n\\n- Problems with performance\\n\\n- Frequent updates of the global state\\n\\n- Superfluous re-renders of the components\\n\\n- A worsening of the readability of the code\\n\\n- Simple UI scenarios require work with the store\\n\\n- A lot of superfluous boilerplate code arises\\n\\nThe typical anti-pattern\\nHolding the following in global state:\\n\\n- the state of modals\\n\\n- the focus of inputs\\n\\n- temporary interface flags\\n\\nConclusion\\nGlobal state should be used consciously and in a limited way. The closer the data is to the UI, the more logical it is to keep it locally.\",null],[2887,\"Mutations ensure a single, controlled way of changing the state. If you change the `state` directly, it becomes hard to track the source of the changes. Mutations make the changes synchronous and explicit. That matters for debugging and for maintaining the application. Such an approach makes the flow of data easier to understand.\",\"The restriction of changes of state to mutations alone is a fundamental principle of Vuex.\\nThe problems with changing the state directly\\nIf you change the `state` directly:\\n\\n- it is impossible to trace the source of the change\\n\\n- the single flow of data breaks\\n\\n- debugging becomes more complex\\n\\nThe role of mutations\\nMutations solve those problems.\\nThe particulars:\\n\\n- every change is described explicitly\\n\\n- the changes are synchronous\\n\\n- they are easy to log and to track\\n\\nAn example of the wrong approach:\\n\\nThe correct variant:\\n\\nThe benefit for the development tools\\n\\n- Vue Devtools shows the history of the mutations\\n\\n- bugs are simpler to reproduce\\n\\n- the state becomes predictable\\n\\nConclusion\\nChanging the state only by way of mutations ensures the transparency, the control and the reliability of state management.\",\"this.$store.state.user = user; // bad\\n\\nthis.$store.commit('setUser', user);\"],[2888,\"Actions are used in order to perform asynchronous logic, such as HTTP requests or timers. They do not change the state directly but call mutations. That lets you separate the business logic from the change of state. Such an approach makes the code cleaner and more intelligible. Actions are the standard place for asynchronous operations.\",\"Asynchronous logic in Vuex is moved out into actions, so as to preserve the predictability of the changes of state.\\nWhy asynchronicity must not be done in mutations\\nMutations have to be:\\n\\n- synchronous\\n\\n- fast\\n\\n- predictable\\n\\nAsynchronous code violates those principles.\\nThe role of actions\\nActions perform all of the asynchronous work.\\nThey can:\\n\\n- perform HTTP requests\\n\\n- work with timers\\n\\n- call several mutations\\n\\n- contain business logic\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe interaction with the components\\nThe components:\\n\\n- dispatch actions\\n\\n- do not know the details of the implementation\\n\\n- obtain the data from the `state`\\n\\nConclusion\\nActions serve as the layer of asynchronous and business logic, keeping the mutations simple and predictable.\",\"actions: {\\n  async loadData({ commit }) {\\n    commit('setLoading', true);\\n    const data = await api.fetch();\\n    commit('setData', data);\\n    commit('setLoading', false);\\n  }\\n}\"],[2889,\"RTK Query is worth using directly for most operations of obtaining, caching and invalidating data from a server, since it manages the loading, the errors and the cache automatically. The normalization of data in Redux should be applied when you have to perform complex, cross-cutting updates between different entities or implement optimistic updates that affect several requests. RTK Query is ideal for typical CRUD, and normalization for complex domain models with close relations.\",\"RTK Query is a powerful tool for managing server state, built into Redux Toolkit. It abstracts away the logic of requests, caching, synchronization and the updating of data, cutting boilerplate code down considerably. The direct use of RTK Query is recommended for most scenarios of working with an API, especially for the standard CRUD operations (Create, Read, Update, Delete).\\nWhen to use RTK Query directly\\n\\n- The simple obtaining and caching of data: RTK Query caches the responses automatically, manages the life cycle of the subscription and prevents duplicate requests.\\n- The automatic invalidation of the cache: you can configure the automatic updating of the data after mutations (after a post has been created, for example, the list of posts is marked as stale).\\n- The standard UI patterns: it provides hooks (useQuery, useMutation), which give isLoading and isError flags, which is ideal for displaying the loading state in the components.\\n\\nWhen to normalize data in Redux\\nNormalization is the process of organizing the data in the Redux state in the form of dictionaries (objects) keyed by ID, with separate arrays of IDs in order to preserve the order. That is useful when the data has complex interrelations.\\n\\n- Complex updates between entities: when a user is deleted, for example, all of their comments also have to be removed from the state.\\n- Optimistic updates: when you want to update the UI instantly and then synchronize with the server, and that update affects several types of data.\\n- The effective updating of the UI: normalization lets the selectors and the React components memoize effectively, since the references to the objects that have not changed stay the same.\\n\\nConclusion: begin with the direct use of RTK Query for every server-side interaction — that covers 90 % of cases. Move on to the manual normalization of data in Redux only when your application requires complex, cross-cutting updates of the state between various entities that are hard to express through RTK Query's automatic invalidation of tags, or when the maximum performance of the updates of related data is critically important.\",\"// An example of the direct use of RTK Query\\nimport { createApi, fetchBaseQuery } from '@reduxjs/toolkit/query/react';\\n\\nexport const api = createApi({\\n  baseQuery: fetchBaseQuery({ baseUrl: '/api' }),\\n  endpoints: (builder) => ({\\n    getPosts: builder.query({ query: () => '/posts' }),\\n    addPost: builder.mutation({\\n      query: (newPost) => ({\\n        url: '/posts',\\n        method: 'POST',\\n        body: newPost,\\n      }),\\n      // Updates the cache for getPosts automatically\\n      invalidatesTags: ['Post'],\\n    }),\\n  }),\\n});\\n\\n// In a React component\\nconst { data: posts, isLoading } = api.useGetPostsQuery();\\nconst [addPost] = api.useAddPostMutation();\\n\\n// An example of a normalized structure in Redux\\n{\\n  posts: {\\n    ids: [1, 2, 3],\\n    entities: {\\n      1: { id: 1, title: 'Post A', authorId: 101 },\\n      2: { id: 2, title: 'Post B', authorId: 102 },\\n      3: { id: 3, title: 'Post C', authorId: 101 },\\n    }\\n  },\\n  users: {\\n    ids: [101, 102],\\n    entities: {\\n      101: { id: 101, name: 'Alice' },\\n      102: { id: 102, name: 'Bob' },\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// A selector for obtaining a post with the information about its author\\nconst selectPostWithAuthor = (state, postId) => {\\n  const post = state.posts.entities[postId];\\n  const author = state.users.entities[post.authorId];\\n  return { ...post, author };\\n};\"],[2890,\"Redux becomes ineffective when an application is small and simple and its use adds superfluous boilerplate code. It can also slow the work down if too much frequently changing data is held in the store, which leads to frequent repaints of every connected component. For simple local state it is better to use React's built-in capabilities, such as useState or the Context API.\",\"Redux is a powerful library for managing state, but it is not always the optimal choice. Its effectiveness falls in certain scenarios, and it matters to understand those boundaries, so as not to complicate the architecture unnecessarily.\\nWhen Redux adds redundancy\\nThe main criticism of Redux is the large volume of boilerplate code. For every change of state you have to create actions, reducers and possibly thunks too. In a small application, where the state is minimal and localized in a few components, that complexity is unjustified. For managing the state of a form or toggling a modal window, for example, React's hooks are enough.\\nProblems with performance\\nRedux can become a bottleneck if:\\n\\n- A huge volume of data that is updated often is held in the store (real data from sensors, for example). Every update causes a repaint of every component connected through `connect` or `useSelector`, even if they use a different part of the state.\\n- You use non-serializable data (promises or class instances, for example) in the store, which contradicts Redux's principles and makes debugging more complex.\\n- The reducers' logic becomes too complex and slow because of the deep copying of large state objects.\\nA practical example: the simple state of a form\\nLet us look at the example of managing a login form. With Redux a lot of code would be required:\\n\\nThe same functionality with `useState` is implemented in a few lines inside the component, which is far simpler and more performant for such a case.\\nThe alternatives and the modern approaches\\nFor simple state shared between components at the same level or at a small depth, React's Context API suits excellently. For more complex scenarios with asynchronous operations you can consider libraries such as React Query (for data from a server) or Zustand/Recoil, which offer a lighter API and better performance in some respects.\\nConclusion: Redux is worth applying in large applications with complex business logic, where centralized, predictable state, convenient debugging tools (such as Redux DevTools) and a strict architecture are necessary. For small projects, simple UI state or high-frequency updates of data it often becomes superfluous and ineffective.\",\"// actions.js\\nexport const setEmail = (email) => ({ type: 'SET_EMAIL', payload: email });\\nexport const setPassword = (pw) => ({ type: 'SET_PASSWORD', payload: pw });\\n\\n// reducer.js\\nconst initialState = { email: '', password: '' };\\nfunction formReducer(state = initialState, action) {\\n  switch(action.type) {\\n    case 'SET_EMAIL': return { ...state, email: action.payload };\\n    case 'SET_PASSWORD': return { ...state, password: action.payload };\\n    default: return state;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// In the component: the dispatching of the actions, the selectors...\"],[2891,\"Redux is a library for managing an application's state. An action is a simple object describing what has happened ('USER_LOGGED_IN', for example). A reducer is a pure function that takes the previous state and an action and returns a new state. The store is the single, centralized object that holds all of the application's state and provides methods for updating it (dispatch) and subscribing to its changes (subscribe). Together they form a predictable cycle of data updates.\",\"Redux's main concepts\\nRedux is a library for managing state in JavaScript applications, used most often with React. Its architecture is based on three fundamental principles: a single source of truth (the store), the state is read-only, and changes are made only by way of pure functions (reducers).\\nAction\\nAn action is a simple JavaScript object, which describes an intention to change the state. Every action has to have a type, and it may also have additional information (a payload). An action does not perform the change itself; it merely signals that something has happened in the application (that the user has pressed a button, for example).\\n\\nReducer\\nA reducer is a pure function, which determines how an application's state changes in response to an action. It takes two arguments: the current state and the action object, and returns a new state. It matters that a reducer does not mutate the original state but returns a new object. Redux can combine several reducers in order to manage different parts of the state.\\n\\nStore\\nThe store is the object that brings everything together. It holds the application's global state, lets you update it through the dispatch(action) method, registers listeners for changes through subscribe(listener) and provides access to the state through getState(). The store is created by means of the createStore(rootReducer) function.\\n\\nTogether the action, the reducer and the store form a one-directional flow of data: the UI dispatches an action, the store passes the action into the reducer, the reducer computes a new state, the store updates itself and notifies its subscribers, and the UI is repainted on the basis of the new state. That makes debugging and testing predictable.\\nConclusion: Redux, with its action, reducer and store, is especially useful in medium and large applications, where complex state that has to be predictable and easy to debug must be managed centrally. It is worth applying when prop drilling in React becomes a problem, or when the state has to be synchronized between a multitude of independent components.\",\"// An example of an action creator, which returns an action\\nconst addTodo = (text) => ({\\n  type: 'ADD_TODO',\\n  payload: { text, id: Date.now(), completed: false }\\n});\\n// The call dispatch(addTodo('Learn Redux')) will send this object into the store.\\n\\n// An example of a reducer for a list of tasks\\nconst initialState = [];\\nfunction todosReducer(state = initialState, action) {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'ADD_TODO':\\n      // We return a new array, adding the new task\\n      return [...state, action.payload];\\n    case 'TOGGLE_TODO':\\n      return state.map(todo =>\\n        todo.id === action.payload.id\\n          ? { ...todo, completed: !todo.completed }\\n          : todo\\n      );\\n    default:\\n      return state; // If the action is not recognized, the state does not change\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nimport { createStore } from 'redux';\\nimport rootReducer from './reducers';\\n\\n// Creating the store with the root reducer\\nconst store = createStore(rootReducer);\\n\\n// Dispatching an action in order to change the state\\nstore.dispatch(addTodo('Understand the store'));\\n\\n// Subscribing to the changes of state (in order to update the UI, for example)\\nconst unsubscribe = store.subscribe(() => {\\n  console.log('State updated:', store.getState());\\n});\\n\\n// Later you can unsubscribe\\n// unsubscribe();\"],[2892,\"A reducer must be a pure function in order to guarantee the predictability of the changes of state. A pure function has no side effects and always returns the same result for the same arguments. That makes debugging, testing and the reproduction of an application's states easier.\",\"Why must a reducer be a pure function?\\nIn Redux a reducer is a function that takes the current state and an action and returns a new state. The requirement of purity means that a reducer must not change the state passed in directly, must not perform side effects (requests to an API, mutations of the DOM, calls of setTimeout) and must return a new state object rather than modifying the old one.\\nThe main reasons\\n\\n- Predictability: a pure function always returns the same result for the same input data. That lets you track the changes of state and reproduce errors easily.\\n- Testability: pure functions are easy to test — there is no need to create complex mocks or an environment.\\n- Reproducibility: Redux DevTools can record and replay sequences of actions, because a reducer has no hidden dependencies.\\n- Optimization: React can compare the previous and the new state effectively (a shallow comparison) in order to decide about a repaint.\\nAn example\\n\\nIn the first case React may not notice the change, since the reference to the object has stayed the same. In the second, a new object is created and React updates the UI properly.\\nConclusion\\nThe purity of a reducer is not merely a rule but a fundamental principle, which ensures the reliability and the predictability of state management. Apply pure functions wherever a deterministic transformation of data is required, especially in Redux and other Flux-like architectures.\",\"// Wrong: a mutation of the state\\nfunction badReducer(state, action) {\\n  state.count += 1; // a mutation\\n  return state;\\n}\\n\\n// Right: we return a new object\\nfunction goodReducer(state, action) {\\n  return { ...state, count: state.count + 1 };\\n}\"],[2893,\"Generator functions in Redux Saga are used in order to manage asynchronous operations such as requests to an API. They let you suspend and resume the execution, which makes the code more readable and more testable. The sagas use yield in order to wait for the effects, and the middleware handles them. That makes working with side effects in Redux easier.\",\"What are generators needed for in Redux Saga?\\nRedux Saga is middleware for managing side effects in Redux applications. Generator functions are the foundation of Saga, since they let you write asynchronous code in a synchronous style. Generators can suspend their execution by means of the `yield` keyword and resume it later, which suits the handling of sequential asynchronous actions ideally.\\nHow does it work?\\nWhen a saga runs, it hands control to the middleware through `yield`. The middleware handles the effect (a call to an API, for example) and returns the result back into the generator. That makes the code linear and easy to read, without callbacks or promises.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example `call` suspends the saga until the call to the API has finished, and `put` dispatches an action into the Redux store.\\nThe advantages of using generators\\n\\n- The simplification of testing: you can check the effects without performing real requests.\\n- Readability: the code looks synchronous, which makes the logic easier to understand.\\n- The management of complex flows: parallel tasks, cancellations and delays are easy to handle.\\nConclusion: generator functions in Redux Saga are applied in order to manage asynchronous operations effectively, making the code predictable and testable. That is especially useful in large applications with a multitude of side effects.\",\"import { call, put, takeEvery } from 'redux-saga/effects';\\nimport { fetchUserApi } from './api';\\n\\nfunction* fetchUser(action) {\\n  try {\\n    const user = yield call(fetchUserApi, action.payload);\\n    yield put({ type: 'FETCH_USER_SUCCESS', payload: user });\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    yield put({ type: 'FETCH_USER_FAILURE', error });\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nfunction* watchFetchUser() {\\n  yield takeEvery('FETCH_USER_REQUEST', fetchUser);\\n}\"],[2894,\"Redux Saga is a middleware library for Redux, which manages side effects such as asynchronous requests or access to the store. It uses ES6 generators in order to write sagas — functions that listen for actions and perform the logic. Sagas make the code more testable and more manageable in comparison with thunks.\",\"What is Redux Saga?\\nRedux Saga is middleware for Redux, intended for managing side effects in applications. It lets you write asynchronous logic, such as calls to an API, timers or access to the store, in the form of sagas — generator functions. The sagas listen for dispatched actions and can start, suspend or cancel tasks, which makes them a powerful tool for complex flows of data.\\nHow does it work?\\nRedux Saga uses ES6 generators (functions with `function*`) and effects such as `call`, `put`, `takeEvery` and `takeLatest`. Effects are simple objects, which the middleware interprets and performs. `call` calls an asynchronous function, for example, and `put` dispatches a new action into the Redux store.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example the `watchFetchUser` saga listens for the `FETCH_USER_REQUEST` action and starts `fetchUser`, which calls the API and dispatches a success or an error.\\nWhere is it used?\\nRedux Saga is often used in large React applications, where the complex management of asynchronous flows is required — when working with web sockets, parallel requests or the cancellation of operations, for example. It is especially useful when side effects have to be tested in isolation.\\nConclusion: Redux Saga is worth applying when there are many complex asynchronous scenarios in the application requiring fine control, cancellation or composition, and when the testability and the readability of the code matter.\",\"import { call, put, takeEvery } from 'redux-saga/effects';\\nimport { fetchUserApi } from './api';\\n\\nfunction* fetchUser(action) {\\n  try {\\n    const user = yield call(fetchUserApi, action.payload);\\n    yield put({ type: 'FETCH_USER_SUCCESS', payload: user });\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    yield put({ type: 'FETCH_USER_FAILURE', payload: error });\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nfunction* watchFetchUser() {\\n  yield takeEvery('FETCH_USER_REQUEST', fetchUser);\\n}\"],[2895,\"In React applications, Redux, MobX, Zustand, Recoil and the built-in Context API are used for managing state. Redux is popular for large projects with predictable state. MobX uses reactive programming. Zustand is a lightweight alternative. The Context API suits simple cases without additional libraries.\",\"An overview of the state-management libraries in React\\nThe management of state is a key task in React applications, especially when the data is passed between a multitude of components. The built-in `useState` and `useReducer` suit local state, but for global or complex state, specialized solutions are required.\\nThe main libraries\\n\\n- Redux — predictable state by way of a single store and pure reducers. Uses a one-directional flow of data. Suits large applications with a multitude of interactions.\\n- MobX — reactive management, where the state is observable objects. Updates the components automatically when they change. Convenient for applications with frequent updates of data.\\n- Zustand — a minimalist library with a simple API. Creates a store through a hook, requires no wrappers. Ideal for medium projects.\\n- Recoil — an experimental library from Facebook, uses atoms and selectors. Integrates well with React's Concurrent Mode.\\n- The Context API — a built-in tool for passing data through the tree of components. Suits simple cases, but can cause superfluous re-renders.\\nAn example of the code with Zustand\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe choice of library depends on the scale of the project and on the team's preferences. Redux remains the standard for large applications, Zustand for simplicity, and the Context API for minimal dependencies. It matters to understand the trade-offs between performance and complexity.\",\"import { create } from 'zustand';\\n\\nconst useStore = create((set) => ({\\n  count: 0,\\n  increment: () => set((state) => ({ count: state.count + 1 })),\\n  decrement: () => set((state) => ({ count: state.count - 1 })),\\n}));\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const { count, increment, decrement } = useStore();\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <button onClick={decrement}>-</button>\\n      <span>{count}</span>\\n      <button onClick={increment}>+</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[2896,\"Optimistic updates in RTK Query let you update the interface immediately, before the response from the server has arrived, which makes the application more responsive. For that the `onQueryStarted` option is used in the mutation, where `patchQueryResult` can be applied in order to change the cache temporarily. If the request finishes with an error, the changes are rolled back by means of `undo`. That is especially useful for operations that almost always succeed — likes or adding to favourites, for example.\",\"What are optimistic updates?\\nOptimistic updates are a technique in which the interface is updated immediately after the user's action, without waiting for the response from the server. That creates a sense of an instant reaction and improves the UX. In the event of an error the changes are rolled back and the user sees the current state.\\nThe implementation in RTK Query\\nRTK Query provides built-in support for optimistic updates through the `onQueryStarted` callback in a mutation's definition. Inside that callback you can use `patchQueryResult` in order to change the data in the cache temporarily, and then roll it back on an error.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nThe key points\\n\\n- `updateQueryData` — the function for changing the cache; it returns an object with an `undo` method.\\n- It matters to handle the errors, in order to roll the changes back and possibly show a notification.\\n- Suits operations where the likelihood of an error is small — likes, the editing of text, for example.\\nConclusion\\nOptimistic updates in RTK Query are a powerful tool for creating responsive interfaces. They are worth applying in scenarios where the user expects instant feedback and server-side errors are rare or can be handled gracefully.\",\"import { createApi, fetchBaseQuery } from '@reduxjs/toolkit/query/react';\\n\\nconst api = createApi({\\n  baseQuery: fetchBaseQuery({ baseUrl: '/api' }),\\n  endpoints: (builder) => ({\\n    updatePost: builder.mutation({\\n      query: ({ id, ...patch }) => ({\\n        url: `posts/${id}`,\\n        method: 'PATCH',\\n        body: patch,\\n      }),\\n      onQueryStarted: async ({ id, ...patch }, { dispatch, queryFulfilled }) => {\\n        // The optimistic update\\n        const patchResult = dispatch(\\n          api.util.updateQueryData('getPost', id, (draft) => {\\n            Object.assign(draft, patch);\\n          })\\n        );\\n        try {\\n          await queryFulfilled;\\n        } catch {\\n          // The rollback on an error\\n          patchResult.undo();\\n        }\\n      },\\n    }),\\n  }),\\n});\"],[2897,\"The main alternatives to Redux: the Context API for simple cases, MobX with its reactive approach, Zustand with minimal boilerplate, Recoil from Facebook, Jotai with atomic state. The choice depends on the complexity of the application and on the team's preferences.\",\"Alternatives to Redux for managing state\\nRedux was the standard for managing state in React for a long time, but its redundancy and complexity led to the appearance of a multitude of alternatives. Each of them solves the problem of managing state in its own way, offering a different balance between simplicity, performance and scalability.\\nThe main alternatives\\n\\n- The Context API — a tool built into React for passing data through the tree of components. Suits simple applications with a small amount of global state. An example of its use:\\n\\n- MobX — uses reactive programming, where the state is observable objects and the components are repainted automatically when they change. An example:\\n\\n- Zustand — a minimalist library with a simple API based on hooks. Requires no wrappers or providers. An example:\\n\\n- Recoil — a library from Facebook with atomic state, in which every atom is an independent unit. Suits complex dependencies between data.\\n- Jotai — similar to Recoil, but with an even simpler API and a smaller size.\\nConclusion\\nThe choice of an alternative to Redux depends on the size of the project and on the requirements for performance. For small applications the Context API is enough; for medium ones, Zustand or Jotai; for large ones with reactive data, MobX or Recoil. The main thing is not to complicate the architecture unnecessarily.\",\"const ThemeContext = React.createContext('light');\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <ThemeContext.Provider value=\\\"dark\\\">\\n      <Toolbar />\\n    </ThemeContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nimport { observable, action } from 'mobx';\\nclass Store {\\n  @observable count = 0;\\n  @action increment() { this.count++; }\\n}\\n\\nimport create from 'zustand';\\nconst useStore = create((set) => ({\\n  count: 0,\\n  increment: () => set((state) => ({ count: state.count + 1 })),\\n}));\"],[2898,\"When its value changes, React Context re-renders every consumer component, even if they use only part of the data. State managers such as Redux let you use selectors, which subscribe only to particular parts of the state. That prevents superfluous re-renders and raises the performance.\",\"The difference between a state manager and context\\nReact Context is a built-in mechanism for passing data down the tree of components without props. It has a disadvantage, however: when a context's value changes, every component using that context is re-rendered, even if they do not use the part of the data that has changed. That can lead to problems with performance in large applications.\\nState managers such as Redux provide a more flexible approach. They let you create selectors — functions that extract only the part of the state that is needed. The components subscribe to the selector's result rather than to the whole state. Thanks to that, a re-render happens only when precisely the part of the data the component depends on changes.\\nAn example with React Context\\n\\nAn example with Redux and selectors\\n\\nIn Redux the selectors can be memoized (by means of createSelector from Reselect, for example), which optimizes the computations further. Context, by contrast, provides no built-in means of memoization or of targeted subscription.\\nConclusion: use React Context for simple cases, when the data changes rarely or the application is small. For complex state with frequent updates and a multitude of consumers, state managers with selectors suit better, since they ensure finer control over the re-renders and the performance.\",\"const ThemeContext = React.createContext();\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');\\n  return (\\n    <ThemeContext.Provider value={{ theme, setTheme }}>\\n      <Header />\\n      <Content />\\n    </ThemeContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction Header() {\\n  const { theme } = useContext(ThemeContext);\\n  // When theme changes, both Header and Content will be re-rendered\\n}\\n\\nimport { useSelector } from 'react-redux';\\n\\nfunction Header() {\\n  const theme = useSelector(state => state.theme);\\n  // The component will be re-rendered only when state.theme changes\\n}\"],[2899,\"The Context API suits the simple passing down of data that changes rarely — the theme or the language, for example. An external state manager (Redux, Zustand) is needed where there are frequent updates, complex logic or a large number of interrelated pieces of state. Context causes a re-render of every consumer on any change, which reduces the performance. External solutions optimize the updates and provide tools for debugging.\",\"When the Context API is sufficient\\nThe Context API in React is intended for passing data through the tree of components without passing props explicitly. It works well for static or rarely changing values, such as the visual theme, the user's locale or the authentication data. In those scenarios the updates do not happen often and the performance does not suffer.\\nWhen an external state manager is needed\\nIf the state is updated often (on every keystroke in a form, for example) or has complex logic with a multitude of dependencies, Context can cause superfluous re-renders. External libraries such as Redux or Zustand use subscriptions to individual parts of the state, which minimizes the repaints. They also provide middleware, devtools and a strict architecture for large applications.\\nAn example of the comparison\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse the Context API for global data that changes rarely. For dynamic state with frequent updates or complex business logic, choose an external state manager, in order to preserve the performance and the maintainability of the code.\",\"// Context - a re-render of every consumer on a change\\nconst ThemeContext = React.createContext('light');\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');\\n  return (\\n    <ThemeContext.Provider value={{ theme, setTheme }}>\\n      <Header /> <!-- is re-rendered -->\\n      <Footer /> <!-- is re-rendered -->\\n    </ThemeContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// Redux - only the subscribed component is updated\\nconst selectTheme = (state) => state.theme;\\nfunction Header() {\\n  const theme = useSelector(selectTheme);\\n  return <div>{theme}</div>;\\n}\"],[2900,\"Redux uses a one-directional flow of data. When the user initiates an action, dispatch(action) is called. Redux passes the action and the current state into the reducer, which returns a new state. Then the store is updated and the subscribed components are repainted.\",\"How the Redux cycle works\\nRedux is a predictable state container for JavaScript applications. Its key feature is the one-directional flow of data, which makes the changes of state predictable and easy to track.\\nAn action's path from the dispatch to the update of the state\\n\\n- The action: the user or the code initiates an action, creating an action object with a type field and optional data (a payload).\\n- dispatch(action): the call of store.dispatch(action) sends the action into the store.\\n- The reducer: the store passes the current state and the action into the root reducer. A reducer is a pure function, which takes the previous state and the action and returns a new state without mutating the original.\\n- The update of the store: the store replaces the current state with the new one returned by the reducer.\\n- The notification of the subscribers: the store calls every subscribed listener (through connect or useSelector in React, for example), which leads to a repaint of the components.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe Redux cycle ensures a strict sequence: action → dispatch → reducer → a new state → the update of the UI. That makes the debugging, testing and maintenance of large applications easier, where it matters to keep the changes of state under control.\",\"// The action creator\\nconst increment = () => ({ type: 'INCREMENT' });\\n\\n// The reducer\\nconst counterReducer = (state = { count: 0 }, action) => {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'INCREMENT':\\n      return { count: state.count + 1 };\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n};\\n\\n// The store\\nimport { createStore } from 'redux';\\nconst store = createStore(counterReducer);\\n\\n// The dispatch\\nstore.dispatch(increment());\\nconsole.log(store.getState()); // { count: 1 }\"]]");export{e as default};